00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.110
So today I'm gonna talk about my book

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:39.050
which John did a very
generous job introducing

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.810
it's called "Short Circuiting Policy".

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.870
And I'm also going to talk
about some of the policy work

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:47.260
that I have been doing with
some of the recent reports

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.690
I have released on clean energy

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:53.240
which inform a lot, some
of the conversations

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.470
that we're having right now

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:57.603
about how to decarbonize
our electricity system.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.920
So the challenge that
we face is very large.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:06.340
We have to clean up our
electricity system quickly

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.520
in order to decarbonize our economy.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.160
I often say that cleaning
up the electricity system

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.850
is the first linchpin in
economy-wide decarbonization.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:15.970
Why is that?

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.380
Well, if we do clean up
our electricity system

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.640
it allows us to electrify
large parts of transportation

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.410
through things like
electric vehicles, buildings

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:27.420
through things like heat
pumps and induction stoves

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.210
and also parts of heavy industry.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:31.710
And when we add up all these sectors

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:35.480
we could get two emissions
reductions of between 70 to 80%

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:38.890
through clean electricity
and electrification.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:41.610
So before the last couple
of years, we were talking

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.950
about 2050 as the timeline
for clean electricity.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.270
And that was because we were talking

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.740
about economy-wide decarbonization
on those timelines.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.270
And you can see here that
if we set a benchmark

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:55.810
from the year 2000, and we
draw a straight line up to 2050

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.590
then essentially each year
clean power has to grow

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.010
by two percentage points annually.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.680
And although this ends in 2018

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.300
the data from 2020 is actually 40%.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.450
So we're directly on track now

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:09.530
but what you should notice is
that we've been really living

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.330
on borrowed time with our
nuclear and hydro power fleet

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.430
which have been largely flat.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.160
And in the case of the nuclear fleet

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.820
have a large potential to be declining.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.230
And so we actually need to renewables

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.290
to be growing much faster in order

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.450
to keep pace with this timeline.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:27.530
Now, this isn't the timeline
we're talking about anymore.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:31.290
We're talking about
decarbonizing the grid by 2035.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.940
This is what president Biden
campaigned, and one on.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.660
And it's very active area of
discussion in policy right now.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.370
So under that scenario, we actually need

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:40.880
to be growing clean electricity

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.590
by about four percentage
points every year.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:47.280
And that doesn't even take
into account the growing load

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.550
that will be necessary to
deal with electrification.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.040
So that four percentage
points could be masking,

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.010
you know massive growth in the
overall electricity system,

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.900
meaning that it, we don't just
need really 100% clean power.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.320
We need like 200% clean power.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.610
And so that could be rather
than four percentage points

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:06.600
on a 100% basis.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.160
It could be like eight percentage
points on a 200% basis.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.250
So there's a lot to be doing very quickly

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:14.980
if you're interested in these arguments

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:16.410
and trying to kind of get a sense

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:17.870
of what I'm talking about here

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:18.910
actually made a video,

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.230
which summarizes this
argument from my book

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:22.740
it's called the Narwhal Curve,

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.230
and we released it with Grist.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.290
And it's quite a nice
little three-minute video

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:28.760
which kind of gives you
the basics of these ideas

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.240
and can be used in classes
or shared with other people

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:34.140
who maybe don't know a lot
about the electricity system

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.910
Now my book looks at the state level.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:42.460
And that is because the federal
government has really failed

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:46.290
in terms of amplifying clean electricity

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:49.890
throughout the 1990s advocates,
try to pass and fail to pass

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:51.440
renewable portfolio standards

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.480
which are the main policy
I'll be talking about today

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.790
sometimes called clean
electricity standards.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.050
They're very similar.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.180
What it is, is a policy
that says by a certain year,

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.320
like 2035 we have to
have a certain percentage

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.360
of our electricity
system from either clean

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:11.110
or renewable sources, like
100% clean electricity by 2035.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.470
So these renewable portfolio
standards were developed

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:17.060
in the 1990s and advocates
were trying to get one passed

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:19.470
through Congress, but they failed.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.910
And I tell the history of
those efforts in the book.

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:25.080
Now, the closest we have come
was just over a decade ago.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.390
In the Waxman-Markey
Bill which you may know

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:30.890
is the very large climate
bill that passed the house,

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:32.280
but did not pass the Senate.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:33.500
And that policy included

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.420
a federal renewable portfolio standard

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:38.560
for 20% renewable power by 2020.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.070
And it actually had a provision
that allowed 8% of that

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.930
to be met with energy efficiency.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:46.930
So that policy, when I
looked back on it for my book

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.210
it wasn't considered ambitious at the time

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:51.470
but what's so interesting is
that when we look back on it,

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.020
had we pass that policy

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:56.020
we actually would have been
further along than we are today.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:02.360
No, of course if Biden Harris'
campaign was really focused

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:05.070
on 100% clean electricity by 2035.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:07.100
And this is something
that the administration

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:08.030
has also signaled.

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:10.263
It is planning on working
towards this year.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.540
So in the absence of federal
action on clean power

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:16.840
States have really been leading the way

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:17.820
and throughout the 90s

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.160
and early 2000s, a majority of States

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.020
passed renewable portfolio standards.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.920
And then later on expanded
them to higher percentages

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.950
and expanded them to include clean power,

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:31.160
which usually includes nuclear
at the carbon free resource

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:32.980
potentially carbon
capture and sequestration

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:34.810
although that largely does not exist

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:36.460
and sort of other eligible resources

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.143
outside of just renewables.

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:42.050
In addition, States passed
a policy called Net Metering

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:44.140
sometimes called Net Energy Metering.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:47.560
This is a policy that allows
you to put solar on your roof

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.290
and feed the extra kilowatt hours you make

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:54.950
from that resource onto
the grid and to be paid

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:56.700
for that supply.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.450
And what rate do you get paid at?

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:01.310
Well, you get paid the same amount

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.090
that you'd be buying electricity.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:04.390
So if you buy electricity

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:07.310
for, let's say 15 cents a kilowatt hour,

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:09.720
you would also be paid
15 cents a kilowatt hour.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.350
And that's why it's net, right?

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:12.580
It's the difference between the amount

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:14.020
of kilowatt hours you bought in a month

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.840
versus the amount you put onto the grid.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:17.780
And that would be your bill.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:20.240
And as you can see here, again,
the vast majority of States

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.970
I believe the number is
42 passed these policies

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.803
throughout the 90s and early 2000s.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.740
Now this effort has only
amplified in the last few years,

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:31.130
and we are now in a situation

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:33.350
where more than one in
three Americans live

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:36.970
in a place today
targeting 100% clean power

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.350
whether that's the States
that I'm showing here

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:42.430
or many cities across the country as well.

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:46.040
So you can see here that many
States have passed policies

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:48.310
targeting 100% clean power

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:51.140
with the exception of
Washington and Colorado.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:54.790
None of them are really as
ambitious as the federal policy.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:58.330
Colorado has a plan for
80% clean power by 2030

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.940
which is directly on the
pathway to 100% clean by 2035

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:05.010
and Washington state with it's
large hydro-power resources

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.230
is quite ahead of the curve here.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.810
But the federal policy
that we're talking about

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:10.330
would really ramp up this ambition

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.470
and notably apply it to
the whole country rather

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:15.470
than having this sort
of piecemeal approach

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:16.633
that we've had today.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.890
Now, what my book looks at is the backlash

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:22.420
that has come from interest groups

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:23.910
and even some members of the public

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:25.500
although largely interest groups

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.860
against the clean energy transition.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:29.820
And what I show is that
fossil fuel companies

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:31.670
and electric utilities are resisting

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:32.870
the clean energy transition

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:35.490
in a wide variety of
ways then and the book

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:38.730
is meant to kind of
describe and categorize

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:41.790
and show the tactics that
these industries are using

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:44.980
to try to undermine what we
would call policy feedback.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.400
So it's possible that many
in this group are familiar

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:51.050
with the term of policy
feedback, but just as a refresher

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.720
typically in political
science and public policy work

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:56.620
we think about how politics
as the independent variable

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:59.100
such as the election of president Biden

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.820
this fall reshapes the political landscape

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:03.080
and makes policies such

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.830
as this clean electricity
standard possible.

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:08.490
So we're thinking about politics
as the independent variable

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:11.270
and policy as the dependent variable.

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:13.240
Now, we don't have to think in that way.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.090
We can think about how policy itself,

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:17.270
such as the passage of a
clean electricity standard

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:20.400
or renewable portfolio
standard in a given state

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:22.030
can reshape the political environment.

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:24.010
By for example, creating new advocates

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:25.460
like clean energy companies

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:27.410
or bolstering environmental groups,

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:29.330
and that they can come back

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:32.830
in later rounds and
further expand the policy.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:35.620
So we're thinking about how
policy is reshaping politics

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:39.340
and then how politics can
then shift to expand policy.

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:40.440
And under that scenario,

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:43.350
we get a positive policy
feedback cycle, right?

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:46.530
An amplifying cycle where
a small policy at time one

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:50.450
grows over time to be a
bigger policy at time two.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:53.110
And that was the theory behind
a lot of these policies.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.810
That we would get a first
renewable portfolio standard

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:56.750
in a given state.

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:59.480
We would be able to increase that target,

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:00.647
bring forward that timeline,

00:12:00.647 --> 00:12:03.230
and then maybe even pass one federally.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:05.450
But that isn't exactly how
things have played out.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:08.800
And that's because what I argue
is that the interest groups

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.490
opposed to your policy can actually learn

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.160
through that implementation of laws.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:16.460
And they don't just kind of
go away that incumbents remain

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:18.980
in the political environment
and that they can try

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:20.760
to undermine policy feedback.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:21.660
How do they do that?

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:23.110
Well, I'm gonna talk about that in detail,

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:26.020
but some of the ways that
I describe in the book

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:27.600
are things like lobbying

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:29.330
what I call implementation resistance

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:32.270
which is resisting policies
at implementation bodies.

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.880
In my case, public utility commissions,

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:36.530
primaries and elections,

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:38.850
particularly how the Republican party

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:41.310
has been pulled away from
climate and clean energy

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:44.320
through primary challenges astroturfing

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:47.250
and outside lobbying
as well as court cases.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:48.160
And if you don't know

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:50.570
the terms astroturfing and
outside lobbying again,

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.010
these are policy and
political science terms.

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.730
Astroturfing is fake grassroots campaigns

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:57.620
where you pretend you're
a grassroots organization

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:58.610
when you're not.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:01.500
And outside lobbying as a
concept from Ken Coleman,

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:04.360
which is about how interest
groups can organize the public

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.300
to amplify their own interests right?

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:09.450
So one maybe positive
example of that would be

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:12.290
if you're a solar company
that builds rooftop, solar

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:14.100
maybe you're gonna organize your customers

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:17.833
to lobby on behalf of
you in a policy fight.

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:23.403
Okay, so you probably know
that bills become laws, right?

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:28.720
But policy feedback studies
ask what happens next?

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:30.680
What happens after a bill becomes a law?

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.700
And we are interested in
looking at how policies

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:36.440
can reshape the political environment.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.390
I want to argue is that
during implementation

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:42.440
that's when policy
feedback really takes hold

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:45.290
because that's when
resources are redistributed

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:46.900
between advocates and opponents.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:49.560
And so the opponent interest
groups theoretically

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:51.810
lose some of their power and resources

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:54.360
and the supportive interest
groups, theoretically grow

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:56.070
over time right?

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:57.830
Now, what do these interest groups do?

00:13:57.830 --> 00:13:59.010
Well, they don't just lay down

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:00.470
and die in the case of opponents.

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:03.720
And the advocates might
try to expand the policy

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:05.770
so that either go directly

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:08.650
to the legislators to try to shift policy.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:12.200
And that would be a very
clear policy feedback cycle.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:14.310
But as we know, from
the workup, for example,

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:16.440
Schattschneider, a really important figure

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.230
in policy work and political science.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:21.980
They can also expand the scope of conflict

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:26.120
by bringing in for example, the
public as part of the fight.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:27.870
And that is something
that electric utilities

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.900
and fossil fuel companies
have been doing in the case

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:31.380
of clean energy.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:33.950
So we're going to give
you two examples of that.

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:37.060
In Kansas which is the
headquarters of Koch Industries

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:39.630
a major fossil fuel and
petrochemical company run

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:41.070
by the Koch brothers.

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:44.070
One of whom was now deceased.

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:47.580
You know, they had a
renewable portfolio standard

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:50.710
and it was doing quite
well as you can imagine,

00:14:50.710 --> 00:14:53.540
Kansas is a very windy state.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:57.130
But Koch Industries wanted
to roll back this policy.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:59.770
And so all of a sudden
this group popped up

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:02.170
called the Kansas Seniors Alliance.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:04.120
And these flyers started going around

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:07.070
saying that call your representative,

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:08.820
tell them to appeal this mandate.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:11.570
So terrible, it's gonna
Jack up electricity bills

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:14.210
for all these Kansas
seniors who are struggling.

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:19.210
So a Lawyer was the person
who actually registered

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.290
this 501C3 organization.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:24.010
And they wanted to know, you know

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.430
who was behind this organization.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:28.710
And so what they did was they went

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:32.400
to that lawyer and they said,
hey, this was a journalist.

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:35.040
They said, "Hey, who are you working for?

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:36.580
Like, who's the Kansas Seniors Alliance?

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:38.070
There's no website, you know,

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:40.020
I'm just curious, who's involved here?"

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:41.843
And the lawyer said,

00:15:41.843 --> 00:15:43.190
"Oh I don't work for the
Kansas Seniors Alliance.

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:45.070
I work for Americans for Prosperity."

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:48.790
which you may know is a kind
of public facing a front group

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:51.410
for Koch Industries, the corporation.

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:56.400
And it turns out that basically
they had formed this group

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.740
on behalf of Americans for Prosperity.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.100
It wasn't just like a
spontaneous upwelling

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:02.393
of Kansas seniors.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:07.127
Now here's an even more nefarious example

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.710
of astroturfing that
comes from New Orleans.

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:16.330
In New Orleans the utility Entergy

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:18.627
wanted to build a new gas plant.

00:16:18.627 --> 00:16:22.180
And in order to do that,
they had to get a permit

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:25.610
in this case, from the city
council in New Orleans.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:27.680
And you can see there was
a city council meeting

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:28.970
and a lot of people showed up.

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:31.030
A lot of people were
wearing the same shirt.

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:32.320
They had these signs.

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:34.840
Typically when you have
a grassroots movement

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:37.500
and organization, the
signs are sort of handmade,

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:38.760
they're colorful, et cetera.

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:41.260
But these ones were all
printed out in a mass way.

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:44.070
And these people got up to
the podium and they spoke

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:45.760
and they said, "We want
to build this gas plant.

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:47.320
That's what we think should happen."

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:50.090
So some journalists went up
to these people afterwards

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:50.923
and they said, you know,

00:16:50.923 --> 00:16:53.700
"Hey why do you support the gas plant?"

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:56.260
And they said, "Oh, I don't
support the gas plant.

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:58.020
I'm an out of work actor.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:16:59.590
And I was actually paid to come

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:03.480
to this meeting and to
speak up in this way.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:05.260
And I was actually paid an extra money

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:07.640
if I was given a speaking role."

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:11.330
And it turns out that the
utility had spent $27,000

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:14.610
on a PR firm who had gone
and hired all these actors

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:17.620
to show up to this meeting
and pretend to be the public.

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:19.930
And of course this raises serious concerns

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:22.440
about sort of democratic representation

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:24.640
and participation when a meeting

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.770
which has only a certain amount
of time and a certain amount

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:30.330
of space for voices is being
taken over by front groups.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:32.110
And I'll say, this is
actually happening right now

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:35.340
in my community of Santa
Barbara, where a front group

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:37.430
for a gas company has been going

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:40.980
to city council meetings to try
to block the electrification

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:42.900
of new buildings in the city.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.780
And it's really been taking up time

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:46.400
from the public from participating.

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:48.350
And they've also been texting people

00:17:48.350 --> 00:17:49.220
throughout the community

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:52.220
to spreading misinformation
about what this policy is about.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:55.730
Now it's not just the public

00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:57.660
that gets brought into these fights

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.890
over clean energy and climate action.

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:01.640
It's also the political parties.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:04.050
And one of the key arguments
that I make in my book

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:06.850
is that one of the reason
why we've seen polarization

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:10.480
specifically asymmetric
polarization on renewables

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:13.900
is because the Republican
party has been pulled away

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:17.110
from renewable energy by utilities

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:19.240
and fossil fuel interest groups.

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:21.770
And the way that I see that
playing out in practice

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:23.290
is through primaries.

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:25.740
So I'm gonna tell you the
story of Russ Jennings

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.460
a representative in Kansas.

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:30.690
Russ Jennings was a Republican

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:32.050
from the Western part of the state

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:34.010
who was elected to the state house.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:37.230
And his part of the state
actually benefit a lot

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:39.090
from the renewable portfolio standard.

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:42.180
He saw all these new wind
projects going into his community.

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:44.590
It was bringing in new tax
revenue, employing people.

00:18:44.590 --> 00:18:46.120
It was a great thing.

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:49.430
And so when Koch Industries
started to put onto the agenda

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:51.820
the repeal of the renewable
portfolio standard,

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:53.173
he voted against it.

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:57.370
And the interesting thing is
it wasn't just Russ Jennings.

00:18:57.370 --> 00:19:00.446
The first time a vote was
held to repeal the RPS in

00:19:00.446 --> 00:19:04.770
in Kansas, as many Republicans
voted to keep the policy

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:07.410
in place supporting renewables as voted

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:10.510
to get rid of it, 44 to 44.

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:13.490
Of course the Democrats voted to keep it.

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:14.930
Now, the next time the vote happened,

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:16.770
which was just a few weeks later

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:21.150
suddenly a few less Republicans
voted to keep the policy.

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:23.100
And then a few weeks
later, the exact same vote

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:25.120
was held again and a few less voted.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:27.500
So we have a nice longitudinal study here,

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:29.670
so to speak a panel of the same people

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:31.230
voting on the same bill over time.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:33.980
And for some things, there
must be something intervening

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:36.890
in the time periods as like
a treatment that if somehow

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:38.790
shifting the votes of those Republicans

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:40.510
to be anti renewables.

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:42.340
Well, we happen to know
what that treatment was

00:19:42.340 --> 00:19:44.113
because it was leaked to the press.

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:47.220
Another representative named Scott Schwab

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:48.890
said that Koch Industries

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:51.470
through their lobbies for
Americans for Prosperity

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:54.157
we're going to Republican
members and saying

00:19:54.157 --> 00:19:56.440
"If you don't vote the
right way on this bill

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:58.780
we are going to primary challenge you.

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:00.010
You're going to lose your money

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:01.190
and you will find yourself

00:20:01.190 --> 00:20:03.670
with a well-funded primary challenger."

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.930
Now you might say, well,
it's just one organization.

00:20:05.930 --> 00:20:06.763
What does it matter

00:20:06.763 --> 00:20:08.800
if you lose your money
from Koch Industries?

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:11.400
Well, let's think about how a Republican

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.390
in the state house gets funded.

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:15.490
Their number one source

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:19.790
of campaign financing is
the Kansas Republican party.

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:21.390
And what is one of the top donors

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:24.330
to the Kansas Republican
party Koch Industries?

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:26.930
The second biggest
source of donation funds

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:28.750
is the Kansas Chamber of Commerce.

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:31.310
And what's a top donor to the
Kansas chamber of commerce?

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:32.470
Koch Industries.

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:34.450
And the one of the third top donors

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:36.150
is Koch Industries themselves.

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:38.620
And so if a key actor like that

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:41.330
that has so much political
power in your state says

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:43.500
this is a litmus test item

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:45.360
and you refuse to change your vote.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:47.250
You will find yourself losing your funding

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:49.070
and with a primary
challenger, and guess what

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:50.720
that's what happened to Russ Jennings.

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:54.320
He lost his funding and he
did have a primary challenger.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:56.670
Now, in the case of Russ Jennings

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:58.850
Thankfully he won reelection in part

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:03.240
because the wind industry,
which through policy feedback

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:04.710
was growing, right?

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:05.680
They actually were able

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:08.050
to field their own
political action committee

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:09.410
and were able to support him.

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:10.470
So he squeaked through.

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:13.620
But even if these people win reelection,

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:15.480
think about what kind of chilling signal

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:19.010
that sends of the caucus overall, right?

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:20.930
If you've ever read Fenno, for example,

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:24.820
he writes about his experience
of spending a lot of time

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:28.060
with politicians and seeing
how paranoid they are about

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:30.710
how they could lose reelection

00:21:30.710 --> 00:21:32.790
at any given moment, from an issue arising

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:33.623
in their district.

00:21:33.623 --> 00:21:34.990
And this is the kind of thing

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:36.720
that really freaks out politicians

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:40.010
when they think I can be
primary challenged over this.

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:42.650
And so this is how, in my view, we've seen

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:46.360
the Republican party pull
away from the Democratic party

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:48.350
pull away from the
center on climate change

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:51.500
and really become an
anti renewables group.

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:54.160
And it's been very interesting
watching what's playing out

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:55.810
in Texas in the past week.

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:58.100
And I wrote a whole thread
about this based on my research

00:21:58.100 --> 00:22:01.150
'cause Texas is a major case in my book.

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:03.360
Texas was a place where Republicans

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:04.440
were supporting renewables

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:06.230
in the late 90s and early 2000s.

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:10.000
In fact, the $7 billion spending
bill to build transmission

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.130
for wind energy was
sponsored by a Republican

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:16.300
quite a conservative
one from the panhandle

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:19.740
and the wind energy expansion
was also sponsored by him.

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:23.370
And yet today we see
people like Governor Abbott

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:27.700
blaming the boogie man, Green
New Deal and wind energy

00:22:27.700 --> 00:22:30.170
and things like that
when it's not even true.

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:34.010
And what I argue is that's
because there's been a lot

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:38.030
of lobbying from fossil fuel
and utilities in that state

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:41.203
to really polarize the Republican
party away from action.

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:44.530
And this is unfortunately a
pattern that's been playing out

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:47.750
across the country over the last 15 years.

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:50.010
So you can see that in the early 90s

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:53.360
and early 2000s republican governors

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:54.940
were actually the ones who signed

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:56.740
these clean energy bills overwhelmingly

00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:59.720
such as governor George W. Bush and Texas.

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:03.870
But then by 2005, we can't
find any bills that were passed

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.803
under Republican leadership.

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:09.920
And that asymmetric polarization,
which is taking place

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:13.440
at the elite level has
implications for the public.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:15.580
Because we know based
on research from people

00:23:15.580 --> 00:23:19.100
like Gabe Lenz, a political
scientists at UC Berkeley

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:21.990
that the public follows elites, right?

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:26.280
If the leads like Donald Trump
start to say climate change

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:28.100
I'm sorry, wind turbines cause cancer.

00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:29.420
And they're terrible.

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:31.640
And et cetera, that's
going to trickle down

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:34.420
into what co-partisans
everyday Republicans

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:37.080
who are listening to him are
going to think about the issue.

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:39.200
And that's exactly what
we started to see around

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.770
2008 and 2009.

00:23:41.770 --> 00:23:44.130
Now, thankfully, this
actually has recovered a lot

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:47.100
in the last few years, but
with the kinds of messaging

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:49.810
we saw last week out of
Texas, you could imagine

00:23:49.810 --> 00:23:51.440
some kind of mass polarization

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:54.023
amongst Republicans happening again.

00:23:55.660 --> 00:24:00.050
So my book looks at a lot of cases.

00:24:00.050 --> 00:24:02.800
As I mentioned, Texas'
one of the main things

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:03.680
that I went and studied

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:07.453
as part of the work also
Kansas and Arizona and Ohio.

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:11.030
And if you're interested
in some of these stories

00:24:11.030 --> 00:24:13.360
and you want them in
a more accessible way,

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:17.430
I actually turned to the
Arizona and Ohio chapters

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:19.420
of my book into a podcast

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:20.900
as part of this podcast

00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:22.860
that I run called A Matter of Degrees

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:26.660
which is sort of a long form
documentary-style podcast.

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:28.540
And it brings to life
a lot of these stories.

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:30.320
So I'd recommend folks check that out.

00:24:30.320 --> 00:24:31.290
And I've also written up

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:33.423
some of these things in places like Vox.

00:24:34.430 --> 00:24:36.310
Now I'll just point out that although

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:37.970
many people are probably familiar

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:40.180
with climate denial
and how the fossil fuel

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:43.850
industry has spent decades
harassing scientists

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:45.610
spreading misinformation, et cetera

00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:48.890
to the tunes of billions
and billions of dollars.

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:52.810
They, this has also been
perpetuated by electric utilities.

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:56.270
Electric utilities have made
a lot of problematic decisions

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:59.980
in terms of promoting a
wasteful energy system,

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:01.210
promoting climate denial

00:25:01.210 --> 00:25:03.600
and working to roll
back clean energy laws.

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:05.440
And if you're just interested
in this part of my book

00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:08.840
or you want to share it with a
class or something like that,

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:10.863
I published an excerpt on Drilled News.

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:14.270
If you'd like to get a copy of the book,

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.210
go to the Oxford University Press website,

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:20.080
which is just bit.ly/scp-book,

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.190
you can use this 30% off discount code,

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:25.090
if you would like, and
then it's like cheaper,

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:27.360
and you can share that
with whoever you want to.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:30.750
And it also exists as an
ebook, which is cheaper.

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:33.390
If you like eBooks, I like
to read a lot of eBooks

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:34.870
and you can also get an audio book.

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:36.140
They turned it into an audio book.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:37.410
If that's something you're interested in

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:39.070
I love audio books.

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:40.360
So that's how you can get a copy

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:42.833
of the book and yeah,
feel free to do that.

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:47.060
I now want to talk in
sort of the remainder

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:49.890
of my presentation about some
work that I'm currently doing.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:52.390
And some recent policy
reports that I've released

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:56.270
I guess in the last month,
the first was with Sierra Club

00:25:56.270 --> 00:25:57.480
and it looks at what utilities

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:00.090
are doing to decarbonize
their electricity supply.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:02.280
And the second was with Evergreen

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:03.780
and with Data for Progress.

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:05.810
And it's really about
how can Congress pass

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:07.040
a clean electricity standard

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:09.190
as part of budget
reconciliation this year?

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:12.440
So the Sierra Club report
is called the Dirty Truth

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:14.470
About Utility Climate Pledges.

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:17.120
And what we did is we
looked at all the utilities

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:19.860
in the country and we gave them scores.

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:21.950
And what we found, and what we're supposed

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:23.280
to input for the scores.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:27.560
The first was are you planning
to retire coal by 2030?

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:30.130
A lot of utilities are not
planning on doing that.

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:32.180
What are your plans to build new gas?

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:34.440
Many utilities have a lot
of plans to build gas.

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:37.580
Remember gas, fossil gas is a fossil fuel.

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:39.670
And what are your plans
to build renewables?

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:41.780
So very few utilities have plans on that.

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:44.930
And without a requirement
at the federal level,

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:47.020
the reality is that most utilities

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:49.190
will not move fast enough.

00:26:49.190 --> 00:26:53.340
So this is a snapshot of a
bunch of utilities in Indiana.

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:55.990
And you can see here that
NIPSCO, great utility,

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:58.350
actually has plans to retire all its coal

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:01.540
by 2030 has plans to build no new gas

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:04.685
and actually has plans to replace 4/5

00:27:04.685 --> 00:27:06.560
of its current electricity
with renewables.

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:09.400
So that's a very winning score there.

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:11.770
And you can see that the scores get worse

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:13.000
as we get further down.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:16.960
And Duke in particular has not
enough plans to retire coal

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:19.403
is planning to build a
lot of new gas et cetera.

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:24.480
So although a lot of
utilities have adopted

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:27.470
voluntary clean energy pledges.

00:27:27.470 --> 00:27:29.480
The point is that they are not the same

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:30.790
as a clean electricity standard.

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:32.340
They're not the same as a law

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:34.740
because there's no real
way to enforce them.

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:36.740
And indeed many of these utilities

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:38.440
that at the parent company level,

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:42.310
meaning a lot of utilities
have complex holding company

00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:44.390
structures where there's a parent company

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:46.300
and then they own a bunch of utilities.

00:27:46.300 --> 00:27:48.040
And those utilities are actually the ones

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:49.840
that make planning decisions.

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:51.970
So Southern company at
the parent company level

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:56.810
has made plans to you
know, decarbonize by 2050.

00:27:56.810 --> 00:28:00.840
But then when they're
subsidiaries like Georgia Power

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:04.800
and Alabama Power are planning to do,

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:06.380
what are we going to build next?

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:08.350
They are not taking into consideration

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:09.970
that parent companies pledge.

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:12.370
And they're literally
saying in proceedings,

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:13.440
no we don't have to do that.

00:28:13.440 --> 00:28:15.330
That's Southern that's not us.

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:18.550
So when you have a corporate
pledge that doesn't even apply

00:28:18.550 --> 00:28:20.680
to the planning entities
within the organization

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:22.170
what is the point of the pledge?

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:24.130
And we can see that very clearly here

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:26.480
because as you can see right now,

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:27.850
utilities are not planning

00:28:27.850 --> 00:28:29.070
to build enough renewables.

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:32.110
If we look at the 2019
coal and gas generation,

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:34.830
that's up to 1300 million megawatt hours.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:39.830
And yet we're only seeing about
250 million megawatt hours

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:44.010
of plan to clean energy additions by 2030.

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:45.930
So there's no plan to decarbonize

00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:48.380
the electricity system
without government policy.

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:52.470
Particularly when it comes
to coal plant retirement.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:54.810
You can see that the
utilities in our report

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:56.640
that we gave F's too, which by the way,

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:57.700
we graded on a curve.

00:28:57.700 --> 00:28:59.440
So you only had to get 18 or more

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:04.440
to be out of the F range because
that was the median score.

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:06.160
You can see that the
utility is getting F's

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:09.220
in our report have basically
no plan to retire their coal.

00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:12.660
They are only planning
to retire 7% by 2030

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:14.490
and the utilities that
are doing much better

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:16.660
in the A and B category
have a lot of plans

00:29:16.660 --> 00:29:17.840
to retire their coal.

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:20.080
So we know what's possible
to be making progress

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:22.330
just too few utilities
are actually doing it.

00:29:23.590 --> 00:29:26.030
If you're interested in
looking up utilities,

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:31.030
we only look at the
largest oil and gas holders

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:32.470
and not oil and gas, sorry,

00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:35.720
coal and gas holders in the country.

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:37.290
So it's not every utility.

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:41.410
It's 50 parent companies
in 79 subsidiaries,

00:29:41.410 --> 00:29:43.610
but your utility might be listed here.

00:29:43.610 --> 00:29:45.710
And otherwise you can just
look at other utilities

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:48.430
and you can get a sense of
what exactly are they planning

00:29:48.430 --> 00:29:50.160
because although this information exists

00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:52.000
it has never been compiled in this way.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:54.630
And so you have to be, you
know, in a specific state

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:57.400
in a specific rate case
to know what, for example

00:30:00.019 --> 00:30:02.340
Florida power and light is doing right.

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:04.460
But now you can actually
just go to this dashboard

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:05.970
and look it all up in one place.

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:08.250
And I think that's really
a very powerful thing.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:11.000
So I'd encourage folks to
check that out as a resource.

00:30:11.970 --> 00:30:13.970
Now, the other thing that
I'm working very actively

00:30:13.970 --> 00:30:15.790
on is the federal policy opportunity

00:30:15.790 --> 00:30:17.410
we have before us right now.

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:19.620
So we have an unprecedented chance

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:23.080
to scale up the leadership
at the state and local level

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:26.590
in 2021 with comprehensive
climate legislation.

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:28.070
And the way this is going to happen

00:30:28.070 --> 00:30:30.670
is through budget reconciliation.

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:32.770
So for those of you who
have been falling this

00:30:32.770 --> 00:30:35.310
the current COVID package
is going to be passed

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:39.270
through the fiscal year 20
budget reconciliation process.

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:42.740
And then after that's
completed, maybe in March

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:44.860
there's going to be another package

00:30:44.860 --> 00:30:47.730
that will be the bike
build back better agenda.

00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:49.960
And that will include a lot of climate

00:30:49.960 --> 00:30:52.063
and clean energy investments.

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:55.180
And we're working very hard
to figure out how to do

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:57.740
a clean electricity standard
as part of that package.

00:30:57.740 --> 00:30:58.700
These are just the pledges

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:01.840
that by made key pledges on standards,

00:31:01.840 --> 00:31:05.750
key pledges on investments
and key pledges on justice,

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:07.510
specifically ensuring that 40%

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:08.750
of these investments are going

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.633
into disadvantage communities.

00:31:12.490 --> 00:31:13.770
So is this doable?

00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:16.010
Can we actually clean up
our electricity system fast?

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:16.990
The answer is yes.

00:31:16.990 --> 00:31:21.590
If you go to the 2035report.com,
it's just 2035report.com.

00:31:21.590 --> 00:31:24.190
There's an amazing study
which shows what's possible.

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:27.610
And they showed that we can
actually get to 90% clean

00:31:27.610 --> 00:31:30.570
by 2035 and save customers money.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.020
And that's because the coal
plants that are operating

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:35.910
in this country are
actually operating at a loss

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:38.470
but because many utilities are monopolies

00:31:38.470 --> 00:31:40.550
there's no market-based
signal to put pressure

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:42.120
on those coal plants to retire.

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:44.190
And so utilities are
just charging customers

00:31:44.190 --> 00:31:47.120
excess money to keep
those coal plants open.

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:49.440
Many coal plants could
be replaced tomorrow

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:53.050
with a wind energy plant
and save customers money.

00:31:53.050 --> 00:31:54.300
And of course their health

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:56.650
and our air and our climate
and everything else.

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:58.550
And you don't have to
just take that from me.

00:31:58.550 --> 00:32:03.347
The CEO of NextEra, a major
electric power builder

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:06.347
in this country has said,

00:32:06.347 --> 00:32:09.250
"There is not a single economic
coal plant in this country,

00:32:09.250 --> 00:32:10.570
full stop period."

00:32:10.570 --> 00:32:11.793
So this is a fact.

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:14.210
So we released this report.

00:32:14.210 --> 00:32:17.340
It's called A Roadmap to 100%
Clean Electricity by 2035.

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:20.320
And it talks through a
lot of the considerations

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:21.320
in this approach.

00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:23.420
And what we really argue is

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:25.550
that the next two years are critical.

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:27.810
Anybody who follows federal policy knows

00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:29.797
that we have as Kingdon would have told us

00:32:29.797 --> 00:32:31.270
and policy windows.

00:32:31.270 --> 00:32:32.870
We get these moments of opportunity

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:34.500
and they don't come by very often.

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:37.340
We haven't had one for a
decade on climate and energy

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:39.520
and this really creates a big opportunity

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:43.683
to increase the pace and scale
of clean energy deployment.

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:47.530
And the fact is, as I
mentioned, this is really

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:51.160
going to build on the
leadership of States.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:54.260
It's also very popular and
it's very practical approach.

00:32:54.260 --> 00:32:56.090
So this isn't something
we've never done before

00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:58.010
already more than one in three Americans

00:32:58.010 --> 00:33:00.823
live in a place targeting
100% clean power.

00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:04.810
In the report we go
through a lot of details

00:33:04.810 --> 00:33:07.570
about how this can be done
through budget reconciliation.

00:33:07.570 --> 00:33:09.480
The basic rules of budget reconciliation

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:12.640
are that the policy has
to be primarily budgetary.

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:14.580
It either needs to be about expenditures

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:16.760
revenues and or debt.

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:18.510
And so the way we think about passing

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:20.590
a policy like this is basically saying

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:22.240
if you're doing the right thing, utility

00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:24.350
if you're building four
to five percentage points

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:27.550
a year of clean power, you get money.

00:33:27.550 --> 00:33:29.500
We will give you financial resources

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:30.780
from the federal government.

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:32.530
If you're not doing the right thing

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:34.860
you don't get that money
or you get less of it.

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:36.350
Like you're missing your target

00:33:36.350 --> 00:33:39.060
and you have to pay an
alternative compliance penalty

00:33:39.060 --> 00:33:42.830
a payment for every unit of
power that you were short.

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:44.700
So that's basically expenditures

00:33:44.700 --> 00:33:46.680
and revenues into the federal government.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:48.040
And it's actually quite different

00:33:48.040 --> 00:33:50.480
from state policies that
involve a lot of credits

00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:51.860
and trading and all this kind of stuff.

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:53.130
It's very simple.

00:33:53.130 --> 00:33:54.840
So you get paid for doing the right thing

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:56.370
and building new clean

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:58.880
and you get punished if you
don't do the right thing.

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:01.200
And the important thing about standard

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:03.210
over and above just an investment strategy

00:34:03.210 --> 00:34:04.720
which by the way, the investments

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:06.810
which would be the production
tax credit extended

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:09.580
for 10 years and turned
into a direct pay mechanism.

00:34:09.580 --> 00:34:11.020
That's really the foundation

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:12.860
of all this work that's necessary.

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:15.380
But if you just give money
and you put no standards

00:34:15.380 --> 00:34:18.330
or requirements on it, we
will not move fast enough.

00:34:18.330 --> 00:34:21.170
We have to say that you have
to not just take that money

00:34:21.170 --> 00:34:23.060
but you have to build enough clean power

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:25.010
at the pace and scale that's necessary.

00:34:26.240 --> 00:34:27.200
There are a lot of other things

00:34:27.200 --> 00:34:28.280
we have to do in this package.

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:30.070
I won't get into them here,

00:34:30.070 --> 00:34:33.290
but the report does, if you're interested.

00:34:33.290 --> 00:34:34.680
And I'll just say that we partnered

00:34:34.680 --> 00:34:36.370
with data for progress on this.

00:34:36.370 --> 00:34:37.650
And they did a lot of polling

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:39.230
on clean electricity standards.

00:34:39.230 --> 00:34:41.660
They're very popular,
particularly amongst Democrats

00:34:41.660 --> 00:34:44.150
and independence, but even
if you add up the sort

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:45.920
of don't knows for Republicans,

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:47.670
you're getting to about 50%.

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:50.550
So, you know, it's not
a very unpopular policy

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:52.853
when it comes to Republicans either.

00:34:54.150 --> 00:34:56.570
I'll also say that we have some examples

00:34:56.570 --> 00:34:58.580
of corporate leadership like Google

00:34:58.580 --> 00:35:01.880
which is aiming for 100%
clean electricity by 2030

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:03.650
in real time at all facilities.

00:35:03.650 --> 00:35:05.280
You know, a lot of corporations today

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:06.940
say they're a hundred percent clean

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:08.730
but what they're doing
is they're buying credits

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:12.040
renewable electricity certificates, RECs.

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:13.270
And they're saying that, you know

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:14.930
my facility is powered by clean power

00:35:14.930 --> 00:35:16.700
but that's not real, right?

00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:18.620
That's not really those electrons coming

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:22.240
to that facility are coming
from zero carbon resources.

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:24.010
And so what Google is trying to do

00:35:24.010 --> 00:35:26.270
is actually deliver that stuff

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:28.520
to all its facilities real by 2030.

00:35:28.520 --> 00:35:30.630
And that would be huge
because as far as I understand

00:35:30.630 --> 00:35:33.280
Google is the largest energy
consumer in the country.

00:35:34.730 --> 00:35:36.580
We also have some nascent

00:35:36.580 --> 00:35:38.590
utility leadership emerging on this.

00:35:38.590 --> 00:35:40.380
And I think we're at this inflection point

00:35:40.380 --> 00:35:43.360
where utilities will hopefully
stop becoming opponents

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:44.710
to the clean energy transition.

00:35:44.710 --> 00:35:47.250
And we'll see the massive
opportunity for them

00:35:47.250 --> 00:35:49.650
not just in cleaning
up the existing supply

00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:51.600
but in building out huge amounts

00:35:51.600 --> 00:35:54.780
of new power to power
transportation buildings

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:56.330
parts of heavy industry, right?

00:35:56.330 --> 00:35:57.780
The scale of the electricity system

00:35:57.780 --> 00:35:59.070
is going to grow enormously

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:01.530
and utilities should see
an opportunity there.

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:04.480
In Colorado, essentially all
the utilities are committed

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:07.390
in law to 80% clean power by 2030.

00:36:07.390 --> 00:36:09.090
And as I've mentioned, that is directly

00:36:09.090 --> 00:36:11.860
on the path to 100% clean by 2035.

00:36:11.860 --> 00:36:13.900
And Jared Polis, the governor of Colorado

00:36:13.900 --> 00:36:16.010
was one of the people
to endorse our reports

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:17.423
based on his own leadership.

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:20.340
I do a lot of climate advocacy.

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:22.040
If that was not obvious

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:25.230
I run a podcast called
A Matter of Degrees.

00:36:25.230 --> 00:36:26.740
It has lots of interesting episodes

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:28.670
about utilities and other things.

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:32.380
And I also contributed to this
book called "All We Can Save"

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:33.810
which is a really nice collection

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:37.093
from women across the
movement about climate action.

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:40.669
And thanks so much and I'll
stop sharing my slides now.

00:36:40.669 --> 00:36:42.269
And I'm happy to take questions.

00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:49.030
- Thank you so much for
a great presentation.

00:36:49.030 --> 00:36:51.400
So to get started everybody, as I said,

00:36:51.400 --> 00:36:53.810
I think using the raise hands function

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:57.460
would be the most straightforward
way to take questions.

00:36:57.460 --> 00:36:58.890
I know that we had at least one

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:03.100
that came in the chat that
I think was from Daniel.

00:37:03.100 --> 00:37:05.100
I don't know whether Daniel you'd like

00:37:05.100 --> 00:37:06.470
to actually ask your question

00:37:06.470 --> 00:37:07.770
or I could ask it for you?

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:15.900
I will go ahead.

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:18.660
So this question had to do,
and this is probably helpful

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:20.230
conceptually for everybody.

00:37:20.230 --> 00:37:21.360
This question has to do

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:25.730
with sort of the difference
maybe between potential capacity

00:37:25.730 --> 00:37:29.740
of renewable energy
sources versus the actual

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:32.170
sort of effective capacity
on a day-to-day basis.

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:34.360
So his question
specifically had to do with

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:38.720
whether sources like
solar or wind always have

00:37:38.720 --> 00:37:40.740
sort of the maximum capacity possible,

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:42.360
or whether in a place like Connecticut,

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.160
maybe where it's not as sunny

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:47.770
does that potentially reduce
the overall contribution

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:50.040
to the energy grid that
some sources could give?

00:37:50.040 --> 00:37:53.030
So I imagine that this
is sort of a common thing

00:37:53.030 --> 00:37:55.480
that any discussion of renewable energy

00:37:55.480 --> 00:37:57.400
is about sort of how much has to be built

00:37:57.400 --> 00:37:58.700
to meet system capacity.

00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:01.250
So if you could talk maybe
a little bit about that.

00:38:02.620 --> 00:38:05.730
- Yeah, sure so when we
talk about any energy source

00:38:06.769 --> 00:38:08.280
we can talk about capacity

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:10.700
meaning the size of the
projects that that is built.

00:38:10.700 --> 00:38:14.000
So we would talk about like
a two megawatt wind turbine

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:18.060
or like a 500 megawatt coal
plant, or like a one gigawatt

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:20.220
which is a thousand
megawatts nuclear plant

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:22.270
something like that right?

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:26.530
Now each of those sources
has a capacity factor

00:38:26.530 --> 00:38:29.500
which is how many hours of the year

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:32.950
is this plant actually delivering power.

00:38:32.950 --> 00:38:36.410
And that's how we get
from megawatts in capacity

00:38:36.410 --> 00:38:39.720
to megawatt hours in generation.

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:41.790
And so the renewable portfolio standard

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:44.160
or clean energy standard is on generation.

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:46.400
It's not on capacity, right?

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:50.210
And the reality is we have
to build a lot more capacity

00:38:50.210 --> 00:38:51.070
for wind and solar

00:38:51.070 --> 00:38:54.220
because they have a lower capacity factor.

00:38:54.220 --> 00:38:58.130
So typically a wind might
have a capacity factor

00:38:58.130 --> 00:39:01.900
depending on the wind
resource aka how windy it is

00:39:01.900 --> 00:39:05.770
of like 30% or 40% or 50%.

00:39:05.770 --> 00:39:07.720
Typically a nuclear plant

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:10.340
which is usually running all
the time, does not shut down

00:39:10.340 --> 00:39:14.600
has a capacity factor of like
98% or something like that.

00:39:14.600 --> 00:39:19.600
And often a gas plant maybe
has one of like 80% or 90%.

00:39:19.750 --> 00:39:22.900
So that's what we mean by
sort of capacity factors.

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:24.860
And so we'll have to build a lot

00:39:24.860 --> 00:39:28.000
of wind and solar and
depending on its location

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:29.660
it will have higher capacity factors.

00:39:29.660 --> 00:39:32.660
So if you're in a more
Northern latitude, you know

00:39:32.660 --> 00:39:35.950
you put a given size
like a 10 kilowatt array

00:39:35.950 --> 00:39:38.840
of solar on your roof, the
amount of kilowatt hours

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:41.180
or megawatt hours it will
produce will be lower

00:39:41.180 --> 00:39:43.030
'cause it's not, it's sunny there.

00:39:43.030 --> 00:39:46.380
And so the bigger issue is not
so much the capacity factor

00:39:46.380 --> 00:39:47.790
of a given resource.

00:39:47.790 --> 00:39:51.230
It's what we would call
intermittency and integration.

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:54.290
So how do we make sure
that we have enough supply

00:39:54.290 --> 00:39:57.220
at every given second everywhere as demand

00:39:57.220 --> 00:39:59.460
because that's how the
electricity system works.

00:39:59.460 --> 00:40:02.760
What was happening in Texas last week

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:04.510
is that there was too much demand

00:40:04.510 --> 00:40:05.850
in part because when it got cold

00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:07.940
and nobody has insulated houses in Texas,

00:40:07.940 --> 00:40:09.780
Texas actually is like
bottom of the nation

00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:11.580
in energy efficiency

00:40:11.580 --> 00:40:14.200
people were using a lot more power

00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:17.810
and then all these plants
were tripping offline, right?

00:40:17.810 --> 00:40:19.770
And so what happens is when supply

00:40:19.770 --> 00:40:22.850
and demand get out of whack,
that's very problematic.

00:40:22.850 --> 00:40:26.750
You either have to shed
load, meaning, reduce demand

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:28.510
or you have to increase supply.

00:40:28.510 --> 00:40:30.420
And in Texas, they
couldn't increase supply.

00:40:30.420 --> 00:40:31.860
There was nothing else to bring online.

00:40:31.860 --> 00:40:33.900
All these plants were
offline, they were frozen.

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:35.890
They didn't have all these gas plants.

00:40:35.890 --> 00:40:37.230
They didn't have gas on site.

00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:40.690
They didn't, they didn't weatherize them.

00:40:40.690 --> 00:40:43.280
They didn't have any kind
of alternative fuel onsite.

00:40:43.280 --> 00:40:45.320
And notably El Paso Electric

00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:47.700
which is another utility not operating

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:49.900
in the restructured market of ERCOT.

00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:52.930
They had weatherized their
plants because of an event.

00:40:52.930 --> 00:40:56.650
I think in 2011 they had stored other gas

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:58.540
other resource onsite.

00:40:58.540 --> 00:41:00.080
I think it was like diesel or oil

00:41:00.080 --> 00:41:04.760
so that they had another fuel
and they didn't go offline.

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:07.160
So, you know, that is really about

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:09.960
how is the grid maintain reliability?

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:11.800
How do we integrate all these resources?

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:13.620
And as we get higher and higher levels

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:15.720
of what we would call
intermittent resources

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:18.950
which is wind and solar that
are not around all the time

00:41:18.950 --> 00:41:21.810
we need to have more
transmission to connect parts

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:24.240
of the country for where,
you know if the Midwest

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:25.310
has a bunch of windiness

00:41:25.310 --> 00:41:27.610
but the coast don't, we
can connect those two

00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:29.610
and we'll have extra supply over here

00:41:29.610 --> 00:41:30.690
and less supply over there.

00:41:30.690 --> 00:41:32.960
And then we can spread that out.

00:41:32.960 --> 00:41:34.500
So that's why transmission is so important

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:38.100
because it allows for this
geographic sharing of resources.

00:41:38.100 --> 00:41:39.350
And it's also why things

00:41:39.350 --> 00:41:41.360
like offshore wind are so important

00:41:41.360 --> 00:41:43.990
because offshore wind has
a higher capacity factor

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:46.510
because the wind is
blowing more consistently

00:41:46.510 --> 00:41:49.210
in the offshore area than it is on land.

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:50.043
So, and of course,

00:41:50.043 --> 00:41:51.880
this is why batteries are really important

00:41:51.880 --> 00:41:53.750
so that we can store power.

00:41:53.750 --> 00:41:55.480
So that in any given moment,

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:58.260
when the let's say a
sun goes behind a cloud

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:03.050
and the wind files down we
can still be drawing on power.

00:42:03.050 --> 00:42:06.820
So all of our grid is going to
have to become more flexible

00:42:06.820 --> 00:42:09.840
and more resilient, but
that's what we have to do

00:42:09.840 --> 00:42:12.610
no matter what, I mean
fossil fuels themselves

00:42:12.610 --> 00:42:14.670
are making our grid less resilient

00:42:14.670 --> 00:42:16.590
because they're leading to climate change

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:19.840
which is leading to extreme
weather events, like heat waves

00:42:19.840 --> 00:42:22.750
and wildfires that are
destabilizing our grid.

00:42:22.750 --> 00:42:25.950
So we can't continue in the
system that we've been in.

00:42:25.950 --> 00:42:28.360
Fundamentally, that's
creating an enormous amount

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:30.810
of instability in our electricity system

00:42:30.810 --> 00:42:33.750
through the impacts of
climate change itself.

00:42:33.750 --> 00:42:35.990
So hopefully that's a little bit helpful.

00:42:35.990 --> 00:42:38.090
And if folks are interested
in sort of getting a 101

00:42:38.090 --> 00:42:40.230
on energy, there's a book that's quite old

00:42:40.230 --> 00:42:41.570
but I think it's still quite good,

00:42:41.570 --> 00:42:42.990
and it's free on the internet,

00:42:42.990 --> 00:42:45.430
it's called Sustainable
"Energy Without the Hot Air"

00:42:45.430 --> 00:42:47.070
and it's by David MacKay.

00:42:47.070 --> 00:42:48.510
And you can download that

00:42:48.510 --> 00:42:51.340
and you can learn some basic facts about

00:42:51.340 --> 00:42:52.770
some of these things
that I'm talking about

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:54.350
and kind of get the 101

00:42:54.350 --> 00:42:56.253
of different energy sources.

00:42:57.830 --> 00:42:59.900
- That sounds great thanks so much.

00:42:59.900 --> 00:43:02.350
So another question that has been put

00:43:02.350 --> 00:43:03.660
in our chat that I'll read,

00:43:03.660 --> 00:43:06.490
and maybe if Sebastian who's asking,

00:43:06.490 --> 00:43:08.030
it would like to follow up.

00:43:08.030 --> 00:43:11.350
So he asks your analysis today seems

00:43:11.350 --> 00:43:14.600
to focus a lot on
investor owned utilities.

00:43:14.600 --> 00:43:17.090
And he's wondering whether your work looks

00:43:17.090 --> 00:43:19.810
at public owned utilities as well

00:43:19.810 --> 00:43:21.430
and or energy production

00:43:21.430 --> 00:43:23.390
and their progress on renewable goals.

00:43:23.390 --> 00:43:25.390
And maybe whether there's a distinction.

00:43:26.290 --> 00:43:31.290
- Yeah, so the reality is that
there's a view amongst some

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:35.330
in the left that public
power will be better

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:37.820
than if we so we already
have in this country

00:43:37.820 --> 00:43:39.963
about four different types of utilities.

00:43:40.850 --> 00:43:43.240
One is the investor owned, well I think

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:45.771
I have to do more actually
than just four but,

00:43:45.771 --> 00:43:47.330
there are private corporations,

00:43:47.330 --> 00:43:49.120
sometimes they're vertically integrated,

00:43:49.120 --> 00:43:51.420
in which case we call them
investor owned utilities.

00:43:51.420 --> 00:43:53.540
Sometimes they're operating
in restructured markets

00:43:53.540 --> 00:43:56.450
like an ERCOT, but their
companies making profits,

00:43:56.450 --> 00:43:58.120
okay that's the first type.

00:43:58.120 --> 00:43:59.230
Then we have a type called

00:43:59.230 --> 00:44:02.990
public power municipal utilities.

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:04.230
These operate typically

00:44:04.230 --> 00:44:06.350
in cities and they sort of predate

00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:08.390
a lot of the larger utilities

00:44:08.390 --> 00:44:11.810
that expanded to become private companies.

00:44:11.810 --> 00:44:14.870
And they operate outside of
public utility commissions.

00:44:14.870 --> 00:44:18.050
And they're often
overseen by city councils.

00:44:18.050 --> 00:44:21.990
So in California, we
have Sacramento, SMUD,

00:44:21.990 --> 00:44:24.220
Sacramento Municipal Utility District,

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:26.650
in Texas there's Austin Energy.

00:44:26.650 --> 00:44:28.540
So there are different places that have

00:44:28.540 --> 00:44:31.590
kind of city municipal utilities.

00:44:31.590 --> 00:44:34.220
Now nobody's ever studied
the electricity mix

00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:35.650
of these different utilities.

00:44:35.650 --> 00:44:36.960
I have started that project.

00:44:36.960 --> 00:44:38.220
It's very hard to do.

00:44:38.220 --> 00:44:39.960
And overall I think municipal utilities

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:41.210
have slightly cleaner power

00:44:41.210 --> 00:44:43.320
but I think it's partially
because of where they operate.

00:44:43.320 --> 00:44:44.660
And the fact that they're kind of smaller

00:44:44.660 --> 00:44:46.020
and city city-based.

00:44:46.020 --> 00:44:49.820
We have a third type, which is
called rural electric co-ops.

00:44:49.820 --> 00:44:53.150
These are utilities that
came out of the new deal.

00:44:53.150 --> 00:44:55.160
And I write a little bit
about that in my book.

00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:56.870
And there's a book that will come out

00:44:56.870 --> 00:44:59.850
maybe in a year or two by
Abby Spinak who's a lecturer

00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:01.590
at the Harvard University
Graduate School Of Design

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:02.840
which talks about the whole history

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:05.530
of the new deal and the
rural electric co-ops.

00:45:05.530 --> 00:45:07.470
Anyway, these are co-ops that were put

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:10.210
in places where private companies were

00:45:10.210 --> 00:45:14.120
not expanding the grid and
they still exist today.

00:45:14.120 --> 00:45:16.450
Co-ops are some of the
dirtiest energy suppliers

00:45:16.450 --> 00:45:17.550
in the country.

00:45:17.550 --> 00:45:20.780
They have very dirty energy
mixes, a lot of coal.

00:45:20.780 --> 00:45:24.090
And there are big challenges
right now for a lot of co-ops

00:45:24.090 --> 00:45:26.450
in terms of how do we clean
up their electricity mix.

00:45:26.450 --> 00:45:29.460
And you can see that playing
out in States like Colorado.

00:45:29.460 --> 00:45:32.460
And then we have also
federal power authorities

00:45:32.460 --> 00:45:34.630
like the Tennessee Valley Authority.

00:45:34.630 --> 00:45:38.380
The TDA is a terrible
utility, it's extremely dirty.

00:45:38.380 --> 00:45:40.230
The TDA is actually one
of them that we study

00:45:40.230 --> 00:45:41.640
in our us Sierra Club report.

00:45:41.640 --> 00:45:43.010
If you're interested in looking at it

00:45:43.010 --> 00:45:44.570
they have enormous amounts of coal.

00:45:44.570 --> 00:45:47.048
They are planning to
build an enormous amounts

00:45:47.048 --> 00:45:48.310
of gas and they are
not building renewables

00:45:48.310 --> 00:45:50.000
at a fast pace by any means.

00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:54.830
So in the conclusion of my
book and the conclusion chapter

00:45:54.830 --> 00:45:57.390
I get into this discussion
of sort of public power

00:45:57.390 --> 00:45:59.830
and sort of changing who owns these things

00:45:59.830 --> 00:46:01.210
and changing their governance structure.

00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:02.810
And will that solve a problem?

00:46:02.810 --> 00:46:05.470
And my general view is no, it will not.

00:46:05.470 --> 00:46:07.820
There have been attempts
in Boulder, Colorado

00:46:07.820 --> 00:46:10.750
to break off from Xcel for like a decade,

00:46:10.750 --> 00:46:12.083
it hasn't even happened.

00:46:13.060 --> 00:46:15.110
And Xcel, meanwhile has pulled ahead

00:46:15.110 --> 00:46:19.050
and is one of the fastest
renewable energy utilities

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:20.000
in the country.

00:46:20.000 --> 00:46:23.000
Now, maybe that's in part
because of the activism

00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:24.930
of those groups, putting
pressure on that utility.

00:46:24.930 --> 00:46:29.510
So even if campaigns lose
to create public power

00:46:29.510 --> 00:46:30.690
there can still be utility

00:46:30.690 --> 00:46:34.550
in these campaigns because
they can put pressure

00:46:34.550 --> 00:46:36.540
on the utility system to shift.

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:40.770
But I think that the view you
that ownership is a panacea

00:46:40.770 --> 00:46:44.410
for our problems is just
not born out empirically.

00:46:44.410 --> 00:46:46.870
When we look at the
massive different types

00:46:46.870 --> 00:46:48.030
of utilities that exist today.

00:46:48.030 --> 00:46:49.820
And the interesting thing
about the utility sector

00:46:49.820 --> 00:46:52.180
is we have so many types of utilities

00:46:52.180 --> 00:46:54.700
so we can kind of see in practice does

00:46:54.700 --> 00:46:57.640
is this associated with clean
power or things that we want?

00:46:57.640 --> 00:46:59.270
And in general, it's not.

00:46:59.270 --> 00:47:01.350
And it's been interesting to, you know

00:47:01.350 --> 00:47:02.910
watching the criticism of ERCOT

00:47:02.910 --> 00:47:05.150
a restructured electricity system

00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:06.520
in the wake of the crisis.

00:47:06.520 --> 00:47:07.960
There's obviously some downsides

00:47:07.960 --> 00:47:09.560
to restructured electricity systems

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:11.770
and the Texas restructured system

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:13.000
is actually quite restructured.

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:15.170
There's also retail choice
and that's in part why people

00:47:15.170 --> 00:47:17.650
are getting such insane bills and anyway

00:47:17.650 --> 00:47:20.410
but there's also benefits
to restructured system.

00:47:20.410 --> 00:47:22.100
Namely, remember how I told you

00:47:22.100 --> 00:47:23.990
about those uneconomic coal plants

00:47:23.990 --> 00:47:26.200
in vertically integrated monopoly areas

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:28.000
that have not been restructured.

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:30.870
These utilities maintain these coal plants

00:47:30.870 --> 00:47:32.450
because there's no pressure on them

00:47:32.450 --> 00:47:34.320
from market signals to shut down.

00:47:34.320 --> 00:47:36.520
So some advocates for
clean energy actually

00:47:36.520 --> 00:47:39.090
want more markets because
then we can get signals

00:47:39.090 --> 00:47:41.370
into the system to shut down coal plants.

00:47:41.370 --> 00:47:44.700
So it's just way more
complicated than you think.

00:47:44.700 --> 00:47:46.960
What I'm trying to say
about the utility industry.

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:48.850
And there aren't really simple answers

00:47:48.850 --> 00:47:51.670
and I'm very open to,
you know, public power.

00:47:51.670 --> 00:47:55.100
I know advocates who were really into it

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:56.050
who have been disappointed

00:47:56.050 --> 00:47:58.000
