that the Green New Deal
framework resolution

00:58:56.240 --> 00:59:00.670
included clean power as a
really key part of the approach.

00:59:00.670 --> 00:59:04.560
So I think that the
approach with standards

00:59:04.560 --> 00:59:07.860
investment injustice is
really the way forward

00:59:07.860 --> 00:59:10.710
and that builds on the
Green New Deal framework.

00:59:10.710 --> 00:59:13.510
And, you know, States can't really do that

00:59:13.510 --> 00:59:15.120
because they don't have the power

00:59:15.120 --> 00:59:17.420
of the purse, like the
federal government does.

00:59:17.420 --> 00:59:18.890
And of course in this exact moment

00:59:18.890 --> 00:59:20.250
that we find ourselves in right now

00:59:20.250 --> 00:59:23.290
they're in terrible shape,
a lot of States and cities.

00:59:23.290 --> 00:59:25.630
And so this piecemeal
approach that we've had

00:59:25.630 --> 00:59:28.500
which is not getting
us where we need to go

00:59:28.500 --> 00:59:31.450
is not going to be able to
leverage that investment.

00:59:31.450 --> 00:59:34.430
So as much as I think a lot of groups

00:59:34.430 --> 00:59:36.430
can get around investments

00:59:36.430 --> 00:59:39.280
and be like, yeah, it's all carrots.

00:59:39.280 --> 00:59:41.610
But at the end of the day,
there has to be a stick.

00:59:41.610 --> 00:59:43.660
There has to be something to say

00:59:43.660 --> 00:59:46.340
and if you move too
slowly, you're in trouble.

00:59:46.340 --> 00:59:49.070
A carrots only approach will not move us

00:59:49.070 --> 00:59:50.200
at the pace as necessary.

00:59:50.200 --> 00:59:52.880
And we can design some of those sticks

00:59:52.880 --> 00:59:57.000
to make sure that they're
not imposing costs

00:59:57.000 --> 01:00:01.940
on consumers or, you know,
creating more inequality, right?

01:00:01.940 --> 01:00:03.500
Like we don't have to have sticks

01:00:03.500 --> 01:00:07.150
be like a carbon price that
is very consumer-facing costs.

01:00:07.150 --> 01:00:08.880
We can have them be designed

01:00:08.880 --> 01:00:11.350
in other ways where we're
not pushing the costs

01:00:11.350 --> 01:00:13.390
of the transition onto everyday people.

01:00:13.390 --> 01:00:16.260
So that really is the
way that a bunch of us

01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:17.410
have been thinking and writing

01:00:17.410 --> 01:00:20.340
and working on the
standards approach is really

01:00:20.340 --> 01:00:23.440
in line with sort of the
Green New Deal standards,

01:00:23.440 --> 01:00:25.633
investment justice framework.

01:00:30.140 --> 01:00:33.020
- Another question that
we have in our chat

01:00:33.020 --> 01:00:35.210
is about sort of the different types

01:00:35.210 --> 01:00:37.690
of renewable energy that we might need.

01:00:37.690 --> 01:00:39.660
And so this question basically

01:00:39.660 --> 01:00:41.630
is talking about how maybe solar

01:00:41.630 --> 01:00:43.700
and wind especially need obviously a lot

01:00:43.700 --> 01:00:48.230
of land mass or water mass
for offshore wind areas.

01:00:48.230 --> 01:00:51.530
And so what would the role
potentially be for nuclear power?

01:00:51.530 --> 01:00:55.203
I mean, any large renewable
energy revolution.

01:00:56.360 --> 01:00:59.050
- Yeah, nuclear thing I
spend so much of my time

01:00:59.050 --> 01:01:00.930
either people loving it or hating it.

01:01:00.930 --> 01:01:04.130
And that's my time, a lot of the time,

01:01:04.130 --> 01:01:07.830
you know the reality is that
within clean nuclear counts

01:01:07.830 --> 01:01:10.110
it's not a carbon emitting resource,

01:01:10.110 --> 01:01:12.880
but the reality too is that

01:01:12.880 --> 01:01:14.720
we're not building new nuclear really

01:01:14.720 --> 01:01:16.923
apart from like Vogtle, one plant.

01:01:17.910 --> 01:01:22.520
We are likely to be
losing some of the share

01:01:22.520 --> 01:01:24.860
losing the share would, could be very bad

01:01:24.860 --> 01:01:26.810
in terms of if it's replaced by gas,

01:01:26.810 --> 01:01:28.633
which is what often happens.

01:01:29.790 --> 01:01:33.950
So we need to be ramping
up renewables so much more

01:01:33.950 --> 01:01:37.240
and we probably need
to keep nuclear online

01:01:37.240 --> 01:01:42.240
to the extent that we can in
order to extend the timeline

01:01:42.270 --> 01:01:46.520
to figure out how we're gonna
get to 80, 90, 100% renewables

01:01:46.520 --> 01:01:48.920
without nuclear potentially.

01:01:48.920 --> 01:01:53.240
So the hardest part of
nuclear is actually its cost.

01:01:53.240 --> 01:01:55.990
It really struggles to compete on cost.

01:01:55.990 --> 01:01:59.430
And so I don't necessarily see
a kind of nuclear revolution

01:01:59.430 --> 01:02:00.630
coming down the pipe

01:02:00.630 --> 01:02:02.903
nor do I see a clean electricity standard,

01:02:04.000 --> 01:02:06.120
you know propping up nuclear
or anything like that.

01:02:06.120 --> 01:02:08.260
what a clean electricity standard will do

01:02:08.260 --> 01:02:12.670
is not allow retiring nuclear
to be replaced by gas.

01:02:12.670 --> 01:02:15.510
It will require that if you
fall behind on your target

01:02:15.510 --> 01:02:17.930
you have to make that up with clean stuff

01:02:17.930 --> 01:02:21.180
and continue to make on
the pace that's necessary

01:02:21.180 --> 01:02:23.900
because not only do we not want nuclear

01:02:23.900 --> 01:02:25.450
to be replaced with gas,

01:02:25.450 --> 01:02:27.260
we want it to be replaced with renewables.

01:02:27.260 --> 01:02:31.340
We also don't want retiring
nuclear to put us farther behind

01:02:31.340 --> 01:02:32.750
the timeline that's necessary.

01:02:32.750 --> 01:02:36.080
So the standard can do all of those things

01:02:36.080 --> 01:02:38.210
by basically saying, here's
the trajectory you're on.

01:02:38.210 --> 01:02:39.890
You have to stay on that trajectory.

01:02:39.890 --> 01:02:41.550
If you shut down nuclear plants

01:02:41.550 --> 01:02:42.810
you've got to make up for that.

01:02:42.810 --> 01:02:44.810
And you got to keep
staying on the trajectory.

01:02:44.810 --> 01:02:48.943
So that's really the approach
that we have been developing.

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:51.680
And, you know, I understand

01:02:51.680 --> 01:02:52.897
that some people are very pro-nuclear

01:02:52.897 --> 01:02:54.760
and I am not anti nuclear

01:02:56.530 --> 01:02:58.890
but I think there are
real barriers to nuclear

01:02:58.890 --> 01:03:02.930
in terms of public
acceptance, costs, et cetera.

01:03:02.930 --> 01:03:04.700
So I don't necessarily see

01:03:04.700 --> 01:03:06.110
a lot of nuclear coming down the pipe.

01:03:06.110 --> 01:03:08.880
I know others like Bill Gates, disagree,

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:10.930
and who knows maybe we'll we'll need it

01:03:10.930 --> 01:03:13.700
because it is a higher energy density

01:03:13.700 --> 01:03:15.710
meaning you don't need
as much land to do it.

01:03:15.710 --> 01:03:18.100
And as we get to higher and
higher levels of renewables

01:03:18.100 --> 01:03:19.770
we could end up seeing huge fights

01:03:19.770 --> 01:03:22.150
over resources in terms of,

01:03:22.150 --> 01:03:24.670
well why are you putting a
transmission line in my backyard?

01:03:24.670 --> 01:03:26.840
Why are you putting a wind turbine?

01:03:26.840 --> 01:03:29.160
And, you know, there are real trade offs,

01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:31.440
real material trade-offs
in the energy transition.

01:03:31.440 --> 01:03:33.190
And so if people don't want these wind

01:03:33.190 --> 01:03:35.410
and solar projects everywhere,

01:03:35.410 --> 01:03:36.980
you know then nuclear may have

01:03:36.980 --> 01:03:39.040
to be something that's
considered as a society.

01:03:39.040 --> 01:03:41.410
But so far people are pretty anti nuclear.

01:03:41.410 --> 01:03:43.410
So I don't think that
that is the trade off

01:03:43.410 --> 01:03:44.310
that will be made.

01:03:47.220 --> 01:03:48.310
- So another question

01:03:48.310 --> 01:03:50.340
that I have in our queue that I'll read

01:03:51.207 --> 01:03:53.460
"It seems there is a potential conflict

01:03:53.460 --> 01:03:58.460
between the 2035 target
of a zero carbon grid

01:03:58.790 --> 01:04:01.920
and the 2050 target of a net zero economy.

01:04:01.920 --> 01:04:05.980
If States start electrifying
transport and buildings now

01:04:05.980 --> 01:04:09.090
perhaps meeting the 2035
target will be harder

01:04:09.090 --> 01:04:10.560
if they do focus only

01:04:10.560 --> 01:04:13.740
on grid de-carbonization the 2050 target.

01:04:13.740 --> 01:04:15.020
Will get even more out of reach

01:04:15.020 --> 01:04:17.510
with a late start on electrification.

01:04:17.510 --> 01:04:19.630
How do you think this will play out

01:04:19.630 --> 01:04:21.217
or maybe should play out?"

01:04:22.710 --> 01:04:24.890
- I mean, we don't have
some insane electrification

01:04:24.890 --> 01:04:26.260
pace that's going on right now.

01:04:26.260 --> 01:04:28.100
We need to be real about where we're at.

01:04:28.100 --> 01:04:29.590
I mean, I have an electric vehicle

01:04:29.590 --> 01:04:31.160
and I am electrifying my home

01:04:31.160 --> 01:04:33.560
but the vast majority of
people are not doing that.

01:04:33.560 --> 01:04:36.960
So we can say that electrification
is coming any moment

01:04:36.960 --> 01:04:39.460
and it will make it really
hard in the next 10 years.

01:04:39.460 --> 01:04:41.860
And of course the package
will include incentives

01:04:41.860 --> 01:04:43.660
to get that stuff going.

01:04:43.660 --> 01:04:48.330
But we are not at a point
when it comes to EV adoption

01:04:48.330 --> 01:04:51.720
or a building
electrification where we have

01:04:51.720 --> 01:04:53.563
to stress about this at this moment.

01:04:54.740 --> 01:04:56.350
I wish we were to be honest,

01:04:56.350 --> 01:04:58.610
you know of course it would make it harder

01:04:58.610 --> 01:05:00.720
from a grid electrification perspective

01:05:00.720 --> 01:05:02.320
but we're not at that moment.

01:05:02.320 --> 01:05:05.240
Keep in mind too, that even if let's say

01:05:05.240 --> 01:05:08.050
we fall behind on
electricity, decarbonization

01:05:08.050 --> 01:05:10.340
because demand is surging because of EVs

01:05:10.340 --> 01:05:13.770
and a home electrification
building electrification.

01:05:13.770 --> 01:05:14.810
That's not a bad thing

01:05:14.810 --> 01:05:17.500
because we have to think
about the overall energy,

01:05:17.500 --> 01:05:20.550
sorry carbon intensity of the
energy system of the economy

01:05:20.550 --> 01:05:23.520
and what we already know
based on research from RMI

01:05:23.520 --> 01:05:24.660
and lots of other academics

01:05:24.660 --> 01:05:27.110
is that pretty much
everywhere in the country,

01:05:27.110 --> 01:05:29.760
electrifying buildings,
electrifying cars is a win.

01:05:29.760 --> 01:05:31.770
You win in terms of producing carbon.

01:05:31.770 --> 01:05:36.390
So, you know, if we
somehow slow down the pace

01:05:36.390 --> 01:05:38.160
of the electricity system decarbonization

01:05:38.160 --> 01:05:40.860
but speed up the pace of
transportation and buildings

01:05:40.860 --> 01:05:42.930
it's gonna be a net win right now,

01:05:42.930 --> 01:05:45.330
the only sector that's been decarbonizing

01:05:45.330 --> 01:05:46.970
is electricity right?

01:05:46.970 --> 01:05:49.350
So, and the only viable pathway

01:05:49.350 --> 01:05:51.680
to decarbonizing
transportation and buildings

01:05:51.680 --> 01:05:55.020
and getting rid of the oil and
gas chunk is electrification.

01:05:55.020 --> 01:05:57.010
So these things are not intention.

01:05:57.010 --> 01:05:58.610
They're really in tandem.

01:05:58.610 --> 01:06:00.850
As we move faster on electrification

01:06:00.850 --> 01:06:02.800
we got to build more stuff

01:06:02.800 --> 01:06:05.550
but it also is a way for
utilities to come on board

01:06:05.550 --> 01:06:07.340
because they're gonna
see a massive opportunity

01:06:07.340 --> 01:06:10.060
in building all this
stuff, serving more load.

01:06:10.060 --> 01:06:14.117
I just briefed a utility
group this morning and said

01:06:14.117 --> 01:06:17.010
"This is the biggest
opportunity if Samuel Insull

01:06:17.010 --> 01:06:18.570
and Thomas Edison were alive today.

01:06:18.570 --> 01:06:19.840
They'd be like, holy,

01:06:19.840 --> 01:06:22.150
we could build all this
new stuff, it's amazing.

01:06:22.150 --> 01:06:25.040
And so it's like a once
in a century opportunity.

01:06:25.040 --> 01:06:28.100
So I do think that
utilities are about to flip

01:06:28.100 --> 01:06:30.890
and become allies, which will be amazing.

01:06:30.890 --> 01:06:32.930
And electrification is really the key

01:06:32.930 --> 01:06:35.313
to that shift in their position.

01:06:36.760 --> 01:06:38.040
- It's really interesting.

01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:41.060
So I would encourage folks
to keep submitting questions

01:06:41.060 --> 01:06:42.770
or even better raise your hands

01:06:42.770 --> 01:06:44.500
but I'm going to take a prerogative

01:06:44.500 --> 01:06:46.450
and ask one of my own here.

01:06:46.450 --> 01:06:49.940
So I know we have some American
politics graduate students

01:06:49.940 --> 01:06:51.720
in the audience in particular.

01:06:51.720 --> 01:06:54.300
And I think certainly one of
the most intriguing things

01:06:54.300 --> 01:06:57.750
about your book on a theoretical
to me is the move you make.

01:06:57.750 --> 01:07:01.010
To sort of take on some
of the conventional wisdom

01:07:01.010 --> 01:07:04.520
about policy, feedback, path dependence.

01:07:04.520 --> 01:07:06.880
The idea that policy feedback

01:07:06.880 --> 01:07:09.000
could be short-circuited as you say.

01:07:09.000 --> 01:07:10.740
So I was wondering if you
could talk a little bit

01:07:10.740 --> 01:07:14.830
about sort of where
your theoretical insight

01:07:14.830 --> 01:07:19.010
into that came from, how
you noticed this in climate

01:07:19.010 --> 01:07:21.340
and energy policy in particular

01:07:21.340 --> 01:07:24.680
and sort of what it was like
to be working on this project

01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:26.700
that maybe took a very different approach

01:07:26.700 --> 01:07:29.020
to sort of I think one
of the classic themes

01:07:29.020 --> 01:07:31.763
of political science and especially APD.

01:07:33.440 --> 01:07:37.370
- Yeah, so I had two people on
my committee, Andrea Campbell

01:07:37.370 --> 01:07:40.190
who's a very important scholar
in the policy feedback space

01:07:40.190 --> 01:07:43.460
and Judy Layzer, who is now passed away

01:07:43.460 --> 01:07:45.410
and who was a really important

01:07:46.770 --> 01:07:48.750
environmental politics scholar.

01:07:48.750 --> 01:07:52.660
And Judy in particular had
gone to a lot of workshops

01:07:52.660 --> 01:07:57.250
in the late nineties at Harvard
when KKV was being written.

01:07:57.250 --> 01:08:00.590
And she was really into
sort of that approach

01:08:00.590 --> 01:08:02.650
where you're sort of
selecting with variation.

01:08:02.650 --> 01:08:04.480
Like you would a
regression where you, like

01:08:04.480 --> 01:08:06.930
here's a case where
policy feedback happened.

01:08:06.930 --> 01:08:08.920
Here's a case where policy
feedback didn't happen

01:08:08.920 --> 01:08:11.040
and you're trying to like
tease out the differences.

01:08:11.040 --> 01:08:14.670
And so the early setup of
my project was in that way

01:08:14.670 --> 01:08:16.970
because she really
believed in that framework.

01:08:18.330 --> 01:08:20.530
The nice thing about that
is that it allowed me

01:08:20.530 --> 01:08:23.450
to look at kind of the more unusual cases

01:08:23.450 --> 01:08:26.170
the cases where things don't go to plan.

01:08:26.170 --> 01:08:29.630
And in that way, kind of
look at the scope conditions

01:08:29.630 --> 01:08:32.550
around policy feedback in
a more critical way, right?

01:08:32.550 --> 01:08:35.690
Like under what conditions
do we get this lock-in

01:08:35.690 --> 01:08:37.700
and is it deterministic?

01:08:37.700 --> 01:08:40.330
Is it really path dependence
or are there forces

01:08:40.330 --> 01:08:43.100
at work trying to undermine that lock-in.

01:08:43.100 --> 01:08:46.090
And so it's not as if my book is saying

01:08:46.090 --> 01:08:48.670
this is the most likely outcome
for clean energy standards.

01:08:48.670 --> 01:08:49.890
I think a lot of people read it

01:08:49.890 --> 01:08:51.870
and they somehow assume
that's what I'm saying.

01:08:51.870 --> 01:08:55.510
No, carbon taxes and
other policy approaches

01:08:55.510 --> 01:08:57.120
I've probably way more backlash

01:08:57.120 --> 01:08:59.150
than clean energy standards do.

01:08:59.150 --> 01:09:00.970
And I look at the cases

01:09:00.970 --> 01:09:02.760
where the feedback doesn't take place.

01:09:02.760 --> 01:09:05.520
So there's not representative,
there are cases

01:09:05.520 --> 01:09:08.890
like California where
it's a positive cycle

01:09:08.890 --> 01:09:11.130
or even Colorado, New Mexico, New York

01:09:11.130 --> 01:09:13.310
all those places I looked
at that's the modal

01:09:13.310 --> 01:09:17.260
or even the likeliest outcome.

01:09:17.260 --> 01:09:19.080
By looking at the cases where

01:09:19.080 --> 01:09:22.930
what we don't predict will
happen, you know, I think

01:09:22.930 --> 01:09:24.950
that it gives us a lot
of insight in the forces

01:09:24.950 --> 01:09:27.270
moving against policy
feedback and the forces

01:09:27.270 --> 01:09:29.930
moving against lock-in
and help us understand

01:09:29.930 --> 01:09:33.510
sort of organized combat,
condensation in politics,

01:09:33.510 --> 01:09:35.330
interest group capture, all these things

01:09:35.330 --> 01:09:36.770
that I think play out a lot,

01:09:36.770 --> 01:09:38.640
even in the cases where
things are going to play

01:09:38.640 --> 01:09:40.010
and we still have interest
group capture there

01:09:40.010 --> 01:09:41.840
it's just normatively aligned

01:09:41.840 --> 01:09:43.740
with what we'd like to
see in the world, right?

01:09:43.740 --> 01:09:46.470
So yeah, the project definitely evolved.

01:09:46.470 --> 01:09:49.080
And if you read my dissertation,
which is available online

01:09:49.080 --> 01:09:50.150
and some people still cite

01:09:50.150 --> 01:09:52.090
you'll see that there
are different cases in it

01:09:52.090 --> 01:09:55.790
they are more set up in
terms of the KKV approach.

01:09:55.790 --> 01:09:57.840
And I think I moved away a lot

01:09:57.840 --> 01:10:01.440
of from that in the final book.

01:10:01.440 --> 01:10:04.050
And, you know, Andrea
Campbell has written a lot

01:10:04.050 --> 01:10:08.230
about how you know, the policy
feedback cases tend to be

01:10:08.230 --> 01:10:09.070
I don't know if selecting

01:10:09.070 --> 01:10:10.890
on the dependent variable is
the right way of putting it

01:10:10.890 --> 01:10:13.590
but people look at successful
cases of lock-in, right?

01:10:13.590 --> 01:10:14.757
They tell the happier stories

01:10:14.757 --> 01:10:17.110
and so one of the things
I wanted to do here

01:10:17.110 --> 01:10:19.620
was look at the more depressing stories

01:10:19.620 --> 01:10:22.400
even though they're not the modal outcome.

01:10:22.400 --> 01:10:24.100
I think it's really important that we look

01:10:24.100 --> 01:10:28.293
at the cases that don't conform
to our theories as well.

01:10:30.240 --> 01:10:32.190
- So if I can follow up on that

01:10:32.190 --> 01:10:35.040
with another very political
science oriented question

01:10:35.040 --> 01:10:37.950
which again, I think many in
this audience will appreciate.

01:10:37.950 --> 01:10:40.800
Another of the concepts that
you introduced in your book

01:10:40.800 --> 01:10:43.610
that I particularly find
intriguing is this idea

01:10:43.610 --> 01:10:47.927
of the fog of enactment on a policy.

01:10:47.927 --> 01:10:51.370
And I wondered if you could
elaborate a little bit

01:10:51.370 --> 01:10:54.350
on what that is and why it matters so much

01:10:54.350 --> 01:10:58.230
particularly that move again
from the actual legislation

01:10:58.230 --> 01:10:59.530
maybe ambiguity's in it

01:10:59.530 --> 01:11:02.890
to the contestation over implementation.

01:11:02.890 --> 01:11:06.690
Yeah, so I think there's a
puzzle for policy feedback theory

01:11:06.690 --> 01:11:10.270
which is that if we
have incumbent opponents

01:11:10.270 --> 01:11:11.520
and they have perfect information

01:11:11.520 --> 01:11:13.410
they know exactly what's going on,

01:11:13.410 --> 01:11:15.810
why would they ever let a policy pass

01:11:15.810 --> 01:11:17.940
that would undermine their power, right?

01:11:17.940 --> 01:11:18.930
Why would they do that?

01:11:18.930 --> 01:11:20.140
And yet policies pass

01:11:20.140 --> 01:11:23.370
that undermined incumbents
power fairly regularly.

01:11:23.370 --> 01:11:26.220
And so what I argue in the book is that

01:11:26.220 --> 01:11:28.570
incumbents do not have perfect information

01:11:28.570 --> 01:11:30.530
that in fact, no political actors,

01:11:30.530 --> 01:11:33.020
whether it's policy makers their staff,

01:11:33.020 --> 01:11:34.930
interest groups, the public

01:11:34.930 --> 01:11:37.170
they don't know what legislation is going

01:11:37.170 --> 01:11:39.860
to do during the negotiation process.

01:11:39.860 --> 01:11:42.310
And I came to that based
on a lot of interviews

01:11:42.310 --> 01:11:45.500
with people making laws
and various places,

01:11:45.500 --> 01:11:47.380
interest groups trying to influence laws

01:11:47.380 --> 01:11:48.890
and even looking at statements from people

01:11:48.890 --> 01:11:53.030
like Nancy Pelosi and sort
of the congressional context.

01:11:53.030 --> 01:11:55.700
And so I have quotes from
like fossil fuel lobbyists

01:11:55.700 --> 01:11:56.970
who are extremely powerful.

01:11:56.970 --> 01:11:59.830
And in the case of Texas,
literally like maintain a desk

01:11:59.830 --> 01:12:01.520
in the relevant senator's office.

01:12:01.520 --> 01:12:03.630
Okay three strong relationships.

01:12:03.630 --> 01:12:06.040
And yet they said, "If we knew

01:12:06.040 --> 01:12:07.340
that the policy would have done this

01:12:07.340 --> 01:12:09.270
we would have blocked it, right."

01:12:09.270 --> 01:12:13.380
Because when policies
are written on paper,

01:12:13.380 --> 01:12:17.570
it's very ambiguous what
they're going to do in practice.

01:12:17.570 --> 01:12:19.120
And one of the things, if you read my book

01:12:19.120 --> 01:12:20.650
in the second chapter, I did a survey

01:12:20.650 --> 01:12:23.340
of state legislators and
staffers, and I asked them like

01:12:23.340 --> 01:12:25.350
how often did policies do what you think,

01:12:25.350 --> 01:12:26.270
you thought they were going to do?

01:12:26.270 --> 01:12:29.200
And especially in the
climate and energy space

01:12:29.200 --> 01:12:31.710
I found that a lot of
staffers and legislators said

01:12:31.710 --> 01:12:33.550
that they just didn't
do what they expected

01:12:33.550 --> 01:12:34.970
a lot of the time.

01:12:34.970 --> 01:12:37.380
And so implementation is where a lot

01:12:37.380 --> 01:12:40.060
of the ambiguity in laws is ironed out.

01:12:40.060 --> 01:12:42.760
Sometimes that ambiguity is
left in laws intentionally

01:12:42.760 --> 01:12:45.170
in a way that Douglas Arnold writes about

01:12:45.170 --> 01:12:46.430
sort of in traceability

01:12:46.430 --> 01:12:48.670
where you say I don't want
to be held responsible

01:12:48.670 --> 01:12:49.770
for whatever the outcome is.

01:12:49.770 --> 01:12:51.120
So let's not put it in statute

01:12:51.120 --> 01:12:52.870
and let's punt it to
the implementing agency

01:12:52.870 --> 01:12:54.023
to make that decision.

01:12:54.930 --> 01:12:56.300
Other times, you know

01:12:56.300 --> 01:12:58.650
you're not really sure
what policy is gonna do.

01:12:59.694 --> 01:13:04.230
You know people are writing
legislative texts on the fly,

01:13:04.230 --> 01:13:06.090
you know especially these days

01:13:06.090 --> 01:13:09.160
in both the state and the federal context

01:13:09.160 --> 01:13:14.160
these bills stretched to the
hundreds, thousands of pages.

01:13:14.170 --> 01:13:15.670
People don't even know what's in them

01:13:15.670 --> 01:13:16.970
when they're voting on them.

01:13:16.970 --> 01:13:18.700
You see this more and more and more.

01:13:18.700 --> 01:13:21.430
And so that's where
this ambiguity comes in.

01:13:21.430 --> 01:13:22.263
It's it's funny.

01:13:22.263 --> 01:13:26.060
I tell the story of, I think
I told the story in the book

01:13:26.060 --> 01:13:30.180
of the Trump tax cuts, they
initially passed the bill.

01:13:30.180 --> 01:13:32.170
I don't remember through which chamber.

01:13:32.170 --> 01:13:35.210
And it included something
that accidentally

01:13:35.210 --> 01:13:38.660
would have actually reduced the corporate

01:13:42.217 --> 01:13:44.930
You know, the amount of taxes
that corporations have to pay.

01:13:44.930 --> 01:13:48.180
It would have made their
tax rate go up essentially.

01:13:48.180 --> 01:13:50.500
And that wasn't what they wanted to happen

01:13:50.500 --> 01:13:51.870
but they actually passed the bill

01:13:51.870 --> 01:13:53.970
before folks read the bill and they said,

01:13:53.970 --> 01:13:56.470
oh gosh we got to go
back and repass the bill.

01:13:56.470 --> 01:13:59.120
And so they re pass the bill
to fix that problem right?

01:13:59.120 --> 01:14:03.470
There's always so much pressure

01:14:03.470 --> 01:14:06.010
in Congress about, you know, the timeline

01:14:06.010 --> 01:14:07.400
of how we're going to
get this thing passed.

01:14:07.400 --> 01:14:08.570
There's two chambers involved.

01:14:08.570 --> 01:14:10.330
It's a very complex negotiating space.

01:14:10.330 --> 01:14:13.210
And so in the book, I
describe all these factors

01:14:13.210 --> 01:14:15.140
that make this ambiguity, uncertainty

01:14:15.140 --> 01:14:16.680
and the fog of enactment, stronger

01:14:16.680 --> 01:14:19.800
things like new policies
very complex bills,

01:14:19.800 --> 01:14:22.520
technical policy domains, et cetera.

01:14:22.520 --> 01:14:25.190
So, yeah, I think that
this really does reflect

01:14:25.190 --> 01:14:27.430
how policy making happens.

01:14:27.430 --> 01:14:29.320
And you know, now that
I'm somewhat engaged

01:14:29.320 --> 01:14:31.400
in the policy process myself,

01:14:31.400 --> 01:14:33.170
it certainly feels how it's happening.

01:14:33.170 --> 01:14:35.810
Nobody really feels like
they know what's going on

01:14:35.810 --> 01:14:38.280
including interest groups and
politicians and their staff.

01:14:38.280 --> 01:14:42.060
So it's way more ambiguous as a space.

01:14:42.060 --> 01:14:44.520
And you know if you've ever
read legislative texts,

01:14:44.520 --> 01:14:46.790
it's legal it's very complicated

01:14:46.790 --> 01:14:48.380
and hard to understand what it does.

01:14:48.380 --> 01:14:50.900
Different parts of it can
interact with other parts of it.

01:14:50.900 --> 01:14:54.140
And critically parts of federal law

01:14:54.140 --> 01:14:56.010
can interact with parts of state law.

01:14:56.010 --> 01:14:57.500
So when we have jurisdiction

01:14:57.500 --> 01:15:00.370
across federal and state
areas that are shared

01:15:00.370 --> 01:15:02.020
you do one thing at the federal level

01:15:02.020 --> 01:15:03.880
and suddenly it causes these consequences

01:15:03.880 --> 01:15:06.590
when a state policy passes
that interacts with it, right?

01:15:06.590 --> 01:15:09.410
So this is why we are operating

01:15:09.410 --> 01:15:10.750
in a very complex environment.

01:15:10.750 --> 01:15:13.900
And I think that
oftentimes interest groups

01:15:13.900 --> 01:15:15.020
don't know what's gonna happen

01:15:15.020 --> 01:15:16.280
and they get caught off guard.

01:15:16.280 --> 01:15:18.590
And that's how we can kind
of explain the beginning

01:15:18.590 --> 01:15:22.190
of a policy feedback process
when incumbents exists

01:15:22.190 --> 01:15:23.720
and should be opposed to that policy

01:15:23.720 --> 01:15:25.393
ever passing in the first place.

01:15:27.160 --> 01:15:29.100
- So speaking of your own advocacy

01:15:29.100 --> 01:15:31.410
and public engagement in this area

01:15:31.410 --> 01:15:33.750
I thought it would be good,
especially to conclude

01:15:33.750 --> 01:15:35.280
and we have both graduate

01:15:35.280 --> 01:15:38.070
and undergraduate students that
I know are deeply passionate

01:15:38.070 --> 01:15:40.160
about these issues.

01:15:40.160 --> 01:15:42.430
First, if maybe you could
tell us a little bit

01:15:42.430 --> 01:15:46.740
about how you originally became
interested both as a scholar

01:15:46.740 --> 01:15:51.560
and as an advocate in climate
change and energy policy?

01:15:51.560 --> 01:15:53.297
- Yeah, sure, I write
about this in the book.

01:15:53.297 --> 01:15:56.543
"All We Can Save", which is
a nice collection of essays.

01:15:58.050 --> 01:15:59.840
When I was an undergrad, I got involved

01:15:59.840 --> 01:16:01.732
in an energy conservation campaign trying

01:16:01.732 --> 01:16:05.860
to get people to save energy,
like as a psychology major.

01:16:05.860 --> 01:16:07.640
And I just, at the end of that felt

01:16:07.640 --> 01:16:09.500
that it wasn't a big enough lever

01:16:09.500 --> 01:16:11.420
that if we really wanted
to tackle climate change

01:16:11.420 --> 01:16:12.850
and solve our problems

01:16:12.850 --> 01:16:15.350
we had to work on the level of policy.

01:16:15.350 --> 01:16:17.550
And so that's what I've been focusing more

01:16:17.550 --> 01:16:20.530
and more on in of course,
I initially studied

01:16:20.530 --> 01:16:22.170
people making these big changes

01:16:22.170 --> 01:16:25.130
and it was fascinating
interviewing some of the advocates

01:16:25.130 --> 01:16:27.300
who, you know, managed to get into law.

01:16:27.300 --> 01:16:28.560
These sweeping changes

01:16:28.560 --> 01:16:32.160
like a $7 billion transmission investment.

01:16:32.160 --> 01:16:36.210
And so many times that
quote, like never doubt

01:16:36.210 --> 01:16:38.940
that a small group of committed
people can make change

01:16:38.940 --> 01:16:40.210
it's the only thing that ever has.

01:16:40.210 --> 01:16:44.720
It's very true, like you might
feel like what can I ever do?

01:16:44.720 --> 01:16:46.320
And, you know, you have
to build your career

01:16:46.320 --> 01:16:48.160
and develop your networks
and everything else

01:16:48.160 --> 01:16:50.790
but it is very true that dedicated people

01:16:50.790 --> 01:16:53.660
who show up and who engage in the process

01:16:53.660 --> 01:16:55.880
can really deeply shape policy.

01:16:55.880 --> 01:16:59.520
And I learned that by
interviewing in my book

01:16:59.520 --> 01:17:01.240
all these people who made that happen

01:17:01.240 --> 01:17:03.670
or fail to make that
happen, but got close.

01:17:03.670 --> 01:17:06.980
And that I think has
really inspired me to say,

01:17:06.980 --> 01:17:10.110
well you know, we have
an opportunity right now.

01:17:10.110 --> 01:17:13.230
I have skills and ability
to support that opportunity

01:17:13.230 --> 01:17:14.840
and I'm going to do that.

01:17:14.840 --> 01:17:16.520
And one of the skills that I have

01:17:16.520 --> 01:17:18.770
is that I can communicate very well

01:17:18.770 --> 01:17:20.180
to lots of different audiences.

01:17:20.180 --> 01:17:23.220
I can make a technical area
understandable to people.

01:17:23.220 --> 01:17:26.250
And so that's one of the things
that I lend to this effort

01:17:26.250 --> 01:17:27.980
whether that's speaking

01:17:27.980 --> 01:17:32.460
or doing podcasts, you
know, writing op-eds

01:17:32.460 --> 01:17:34.650
making things understandable to people.

01:17:34.650 --> 01:17:35.970
And the other thing that I do well

01:17:35.970 --> 01:17:37.760
is connect people together.

01:17:37.760 --> 01:17:39.950
And so I often think of myself

01:17:39.950 --> 01:17:41.860
as like a yenta to the climate movement

01:17:41.860 --> 01:17:44.240
where people reach out to me all the time.

01:17:44.240 --> 01:17:47.110
And one thing we can
do is organize, right.

01:17:47.110 --> 01:17:48.670
We can connect people with other people.

01:17:48.670 --> 01:17:50.160
We can make connections.

01:17:50.160 --> 01:17:54.480
We can, by working with
others, get new things done.

01:17:54.480 --> 01:17:56.820
And because I've been very flexible

01:17:56.820 --> 01:18:00.890
and not seeing my career
as I write academic papers,

01:18:00.890 --> 01:18:02.640
and that's it,

01:18:02.640 --> 01:18:04.630
I've been able to, for
example, write that report

01:18:04.630 --> 01:18:07.470
with Sierra Club, which just
came out of some conversations

01:18:07.470 --> 01:18:10.030
we were having about some
academic work I was doing.

01:18:10.030 --> 01:18:12.340
And interestingly, I
abandoned the academic paper

01:18:12.340 --> 01:18:14.730
because I felt it wasn't
really relevant enough

01:18:14.730 --> 01:18:15.563
and moving fast enough.

01:18:15.563 --> 01:18:18.880
And then I wrote this policy
report instead with the NGO.

01:18:18.880 --> 01:18:21.430
So, you know, we have
choices that we can make

01:18:21.430 --> 01:18:24.750
about how practical and
applied we want our work to be.

01:18:24.750 --> 01:18:26.580
And at least as somebody
who works in climate change

01:18:26.580 --> 01:18:28.890
and energy and the urgency of now

01:18:28.890 --> 01:18:31.890
I feel that my work should
be as practical as possible.

01:18:31.890 --> 01:18:34.753
And that's really how I orient myself.

01:18:36.474 --> 01:18:37.830
- So I think I think your answer here

01:18:37.830 --> 01:18:39.980
has given us some insight into this,

01:18:39.980 --> 01:18:43.210
but I'm curious if you
have any specific advice

01:18:43.210 --> 01:18:45.160
that you would give and maybe also

01:18:45.160 --> 01:18:48.490
whether it might be different
to undergraduate students

01:18:48.490 --> 01:18:50.820
or graduate students interested

01:18:50.820 --> 01:18:53.773
in climate and energy politics
and policy specifically.

01:18:55.210 --> 01:18:58.640
- Well, I would say
one thing that I did is

01:18:58.640 --> 01:19:00.930
I took a lot of
interdisciplinary coursework

01:19:00.930 --> 01:19:02.100
throughout my education

01:19:02.100 --> 01:19:06.180
at the undergrad master's
and master's PhD level.

01:19:06.180 --> 01:19:09.200
I took engineering, toxicology chemistry

01:19:10.900 --> 01:19:15.630
you know, management, finance,
economics, policy politics

01:19:16.530 --> 01:19:19.310
all kinds of stuff and
hydrology carbon capture

01:19:19.310 --> 01:19:22.210
and sequestration, of course,
lots of classes on energy

01:19:22.210 --> 01:19:25.687
and electricity and the, by taking such a,

01:19:25.687 --> 01:19:29.450
and psychology by taking
such a broad swath

01:19:29.450 --> 01:19:32.560
of training that really has set me up well

01:19:32.560 --> 01:19:35.580
to understand the climate
and energy problem deeply,

01:19:35.580 --> 01:19:37.550
because I understand, you know

01:19:37.550 --> 01:19:39.760
biogeochemistry at a basic level,

01:19:39.760 --> 01:19:41.620
I was in a chemistry lab in my PhD

01:19:41.620 --> 01:19:45.000
for like five years atmospheric chemistry.

01:19:45.000 --> 01:19:47.410
Obviously I don't run
atmospheric chemistry models

01:19:47.410 --> 01:19:49.120
for my job, but I didn't think

01:19:49.120 --> 01:19:50.700
that was like a waste of my time

01:19:50.700 --> 01:19:52.550
or something I couldn't engage in.

01:19:52.550 --> 01:19:54.410
And I did these interdisciplinary papers

01:19:54.410 --> 01:19:57.780
with scientists who are working on models

01:19:57.780 --> 01:19:59.280
and I helped explain, you know

01:19:59.280 --> 01:20:02.140
what are the policy or
political implications of that?

01:20:02.140 --> 01:20:04.720
And so having this knowledge

01:20:04.720 --> 01:20:07.390
in a lot of different
areas is extremely powerful

01:20:07.390 --> 01:20:10.540
for the climate and energy
space, because it is technical.

01:20:10.540 --> 01:20:13.780
It is science-based,
there's so many disciplines

01:20:13.780 --> 01:20:15.600
that weigh in on what we do.

01:20:15.600 --> 01:20:18.180
And so that's been a really critical thing

01:20:18.180 --> 01:20:19.300
in terms of my learning.

01:20:19.300 --> 01:20:21.530
And I feel that it is
something that sets me apart

01:20:21.530 --> 01:20:25.610
from many others and that I
can dialogue fairly fluently

01:20:25.610 --> 01:20:28.000
with very technical people, even though

01:20:28.000 --> 01:20:31.900
I am a political scientist
policy expert, whatever.

01:20:31.900 --> 01:20:33.560
So I would really encourage
people to do that,

01:20:33.560 --> 01:20:35.270
to stretch outside of their comfort zone,

01:20:35.270 --> 01:20:39.560
take different classes learn
basic science and engineering.

01:20:39.560 --> 01:20:42.770
Cause it really is a great
foundation for the work.

01:20:42.770 --> 01:20:45.190
Work in interdisciplinary
spaces with folks

01:20:45.190 --> 01:20:46.530
who are open to it.

01:20:46.530 --> 01:20:48.600
And everybody I worked
with throughout the way

01:20:48.600 --> 01:20:50.760
it was very applied and normative,

01:20:50.760 --> 01:20:54.080
even if they were asking sort
of very positive questions

01:20:54.080 --> 01:20:56.280
and doing very, you know, rigorous work.

01:20:56.280 --> 01:20:58.310
And that always creates a community

01:20:58.310 --> 01:21:01.330
of solidarity where all
of us are mission-focused

01:21:01.330 --> 01:21:05.134
even if we come from
different communities.

01:21:05.134 --> 01:21:08.280
So that's really what I would
say is probably my advice.

01:21:08.280 --> 01:21:11.090
I would also encourage
people to join organizations

01:21:11.090 --> 01:21:13.690
get involved in climate activism.

01:21:13.690 --> 01:21:15.790
If that's something that
they feel strongly about,

01:21:15.790 --> 01:21:17.720
you know it isn't
something that I do a lot

01:21:17.720 --> 01:21:21.100
because in the early years, because I,

01:21:21.100 --> 01:21:23.190
as you can probably tell
I'm a fairly nerdy person

01:21:23.190 --> 01:21:25.880
I like studying and writing and learning,

01:21:25.880 --> 01:21:28.550
and I didn't feel as
comfortable with protesting

01:21:28.550 --> 01:21:29.770
or things like that

01:21:29.770 --> 01:21:31.850
but other people might feel the opposite.

01:21:31.850 --> 01:21:34.750
And I think we've seen just
how powerful protesting

01:21:34.750 --> 01:21:35.720
is to the movement.

01:21:35.720 --> 01:21:38.690
And this is something that
I of course do more now

01:21:38.690 --> 01:21:41.660
and Ayana Johnson, who's one
of the editors of this book.

01:21:41.660 --> 01:21:43.485
She always says that,

01:21:43.485 --> 01:21:45.700
you know the climate
movement needs everything.

01:21:45.700 --> 01:21:47.690
And so whatever your talents are

01:21:47.690 --> 01:21:50.270
you should bring that to the movement.

01:21:50.270 --> 01:21:52.440
You know, if you're write music,

01:21:52.440 --> 01:21:54.290
some people write songs
about climate change,

01:21:54.290 --> 01:21:58.260
or if you you know, like writing,

01:21:58.260 --> 01:21:59.560
you can write about things.

01:21:59.560 --> 01:22:01.600
If you like organizing, you know

01:22:01.600 --> 01:22:04.320
you can lend those resources
to help organize groups

01:22:04.320 --> 01:22:05.320
and get things done.

01:22:05.320 --> 01:22:09.170
And so whatever you like,
whatever is your passion or skill

01:22:09.170 --> 01:22:11.780
that is something that the
climate movement can use.

01:22:11.780 --> 01:22:15.010
And so I would just encourage
people to bring their skills

01:22:15.010 --> 01:22:15.843
to the movement.

01:22:17.070 --> 01:22:18.461
Well, thanks so much for that

01:22:18.461 --> 01:22:20.270
and that's really good
advice for everybody here.

01:22:20.270 --> 01:22:22.300
I think to follow I'm, especially thinking

01:22:22.300 --> 01:22:24.620
about all the
interdisciplinary opportunities

01:22:24.620 --> 01:22:26.470
you have and if you're
an undergraduate Yale

01:22:26.470 --> 01:22:28.860
and undergraduate student at
Yale, just the sheer amount

01:22:28.860 --> 01:22:31.220
of different classes that you can take.

01:22:31.220 --> 01:22:32.900
So one last question from me

01:22:32.900 --> 01:22:35.560
which I'm sure is sort
of on everybody's minds

01:22:35.560 --> 01:22:37.970
obviously you right now are deeply engaged

01:22:37.970 --> 01:22:41.410
in your reports and your work
on the potential policies

01:22:41.410 --> 01:22:42.800
that the Biden administration

01:22:42.800 --> 01:22:45.660
can try to pursue through reconciliation.

01:22:45.660 --> 01:22:47.210
I imagine also maybe some things

01:22:47.210 --> 01:22:50.320
through unilateral action
at the EPA or otherwise.

01:22:50.320 --> 01:22:54.410
So I'm curious, what
do you think the most,

01:22:54.410 --> 01:22:56.310
what would make you optimistic

01:22:56.310 --> 01:22:59.610
of something big happening
in the next two years?

01:22:59.610 --> 01:23:02.000
Or what do you think the most
likely good thing to happen

01:23:02.000 --> 01:23:03.680
in the next two years is

01:23:03.680 --> 01:23:05.220
- Well, I'm already very optimistic.

01:23:05.220 --> 01:23:08.820
I think the Biden and Harris
administration has staffed up

01:23:08.820 --> 01:23:10.840
very aggressively on climate and energy.

01:23:10.840 --> 01:23:13.300
I know many of the people
who are holding key positions

01:23:13.300 --> 01:23:17.000
and they are dogged advocates
for the right side of history.

01:23:17.000 --> 01:23:19.230
So I feel very, very strongly

01:23:19.230 --> 01:23:22.680
that this is going to be
a landmark administration

01:23:22.680 --> 01:23:24.450
on climate and clean energy.

01:23:24.450 --> 01:23:29.450
And I think what I am looking
forward to is the next bill

01:23:29.660 --> 01:23:32.090
the budget reconciliation bill that starts

01:23:32.090 --> 01:23:34.840
at maybe a month or two on

01:23:34.840 --> 01:23:38.400
on the build back better
climate and clean energy agenda.

01:23:38.400 --> 01:23:40.930
I think it's going to be
absolutely transformative

01:23:40.930 --> 01:23:44.200
to our society and I
think we're gonna pass it.

01:23:44.200 --> 01:23:47.340
And that is not what I
would have said years ago.

01:23:47.340 --> 01:23:48.730
You know, that's where the power

01:23:48.730 --> 01:23:50.500
of the youth climate movement

01:23:50.500 --> 01:23:52.060
of so many people from, you know,

01:23:52.060 --> 01:23:54.140
governor Inslee running for president

01:23:54.140 --> 01:23:56.750
also the impacts of
climate change in practice,

01:23:56.750 --> 01:23:59.690
like heat waves and fires and hurricanes

01:23:59.690 --> 01:24:01.970
like the IPCC 1.5 degree report.

01:24:01.970 --> 01:24:04.450
There's all these factors,
Greta Thunberg, right?

01:24:04.450 --> 01:24:07.140
Like all these factors have
just raised the salience

01:24:07.140 --> 01:24:09.330
for politicians so much more of our issue.

01:24:09.330 --> 01:24:11.240
And we're no longer a tier two issue.

01:24:11.240 --> 01:24:14.410
We're really a top flight
issue, not just for the Biden

01:24:14.410 --> 01:24:16.430
but for many senators and representatives.

01:24:16.430 --> 01:24:19.670
So I am extremely hopeful that the package

01:24:19.670 --> 01:24:21.940
will include a clean electricity standard

01:24:21.940 --> 01:24:24.100
as well as other key investments

01:24:24.100 --> 01:24:28.010
on buildings, transportation, electricity

01:24:28.010 --> 01:24:30.100
making sure we deal with EJ issues.

01:24:30.100 --> 01:24:32.020
We're investing in the transition

01:24:32.020 --> 01:24:33.450
into disadvantaged communities

01:24:33.450 --> 01:24:35.950
that are former fossil fuel communities

01:24:35.950 --> 01:24:39.290
or former sites of
polluting infrastructure.

01:24:39.290 --> 01:24:40.840
I just think that this is like

01:24:40.840 --> 01:24:44.140
this is the Waxman-Markey 2.0
without cap and trade bill,

01:24:44.140 --> 01:24:45.330
without the cap and trade part of it.

01:24:45.330 --> 01:24:48.410
And yeah, it's like
the biggest opportunity

01:24:48.410 --> 01:24:50.200
in more than a decade and it's coming,

01:24:50.200 --> 01:24:51.620
it's coming very fast.

01:24:51.620 --> 01:24:55.260
So I spend all day everyday
on that pretty much.

01:24:55.260 --> 01:24:57.050
And I feel really, really hopeful

01:24:57.050 --> 01:24:59.640
and excited that we have a chance.

01:24:59.640 --> 01:25:01.710
And the general timeline
that people talk about

01:25:01.710 --> 01:25:06.710
is like maybe March or April
until August or September

01:25:06.900 --> 01:25:08.810
is when this bill would be negotiated.

01:25:08.810 --> 01:25:11.670
So it's happening, I
would encourage people

01:25:11.670 --> 01:25:14.310
to plug into organizations
as we get closer to bills

01:25:14.310 --> 01:25:17.410
write to your Senator,
write to your representative

01:25:17.410 --> 01:25:20.260
keep the pressure up because
we've just seen the power of

01:25:21.407 --> 01:25:22.260
of people pressure to get climate change

01:25:22.260 --> 01:25:23.563
at the top of the agenda.

01:25:24.850 --> 01:25:27.700
- That's great to know and
that gives us all something

01:25:27.700 --> 01:25:29.090
to look forward to hopefully,

01:25:29.090 --> 01:25:31.500
and certainly to keep close tabs on

01:25:31.500 --> 01:25:32.980
and perhaps advocate for.

01:25:32.980 --> 01:25:34.120
Cause again, I know how many

01:25:34.120 --> 01:25:36.940
of you care about this
that are in our audience.

01:25:36.940 --> 01:25:38.610
So we're just about at time.

